\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Federal Worker Fired Over Flag Sues Government\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Pohle \par \uc2 823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  employee fired after hanging a transgender pride flag at Yosemite has sued several federal agencies and officials, claiming a First Amendment violation.\par \par \uc2 The suit, filed in federal district court in Washington, D.C., is the latest flashpoint in debates over what speech is protected at work and the rights federal employees have in sharing personal views.\par \par \uc2 In May 2025, Shannon "SJ" Joslin was among a group of climbers who ascended the El Capitan granite formation and hung a 55-foot-by-35-foot flag. The group of seven, which included other Park Service employees, hung the blue, white and pink flag after seeing Trump administration efforts to roll back rights for people who identified as trans.\par \par \uc2 Joslin, who is nonbinary and uses they/them pronouns, took part in the flag-hanging display at "El Cap" on a day off from work. The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  fired the 36-year-old nearly three months later.\par \par \uc2 The termination notice, a copy of which was viewed by The Wall Street Journal, said Joslin failed to demonstrate acceptable conduct during employment and the flag display occurred in an area where such demonstrations aren't allowed. Joslin had four weeks left in a probationary period.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  informed Joslin that a criminal probe had been opened into the situation, according to the complaint. The Journal couldn't determine the status of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 The Interior Department, National Park Service and Justice Department are defendants in Joslin's complaint. Interior Secretary \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  and Attorney General Pam Bondi are also named.\par \par \uc2 The Interior Department said it takes "the protection of the park's resources and the experience of our visitors very seriously and will not tolerate violations of laws and regulations that impact those resources and experiences."\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Joslin is asking the court to reinstate them as a Park Service employee, award damages and halt the criminal investigation. "I don't want there to be any indication that our constitutional rights only exist for those at the top," Joslin said.\par \par \uc2 Government employees forgo some personal freedoms on the job to remain nonpartisan civil servants. Whether these workers have a right to speak has been the subject of prior legal cases. A former \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  employee sued the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after he was fired for displaying an LGBTQ flag at his workstation. That case is continuing.\par \par \uc2 To determine whether an employee's speech is protected, there is a balancing test between an individual's rights and disruption to the agency. The speech also must be made in the employee's capacity as a private citizen on a matter of public concern, which Joslin's lawyers argue applies to the flag display.\par \par \uc2 Handling Joslin's case are attorneys from the Civil Service Law Center, a firm formed by former Justice Department litigators, and the nonprofit group \uc2 Public Employees for Environmental Responsibility\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several unions representing national park workers issued a statement last month supporting Joslin's reinstatement and right to free speech.\par \par \uc2 Joslin began working as a wildlife biologist in Yosemite in 2021, eventually going on to manage the park's wildlife data and bat conservation program.\par \par \uc2 After attending a protest against federal workforce cuts early last year, Joslin wanted to organize a group of climbers to make a statement in support of the trans community. They found El Capitan a fitting place to make the point that "trans is natural." The organizers scouted a location to hang the flag over a series of weeks.\par \par \uc2 Yosemite has designated "First Amendment areas" that allocate space for demonstrations and other activities that could potentially damage park resources. El Capitan, with its 3,000-foot-tall rock face, isn't one of the areas.\par \par \uc2 The next day, Yosemite's acting superintendent signed a ban on visitors hanging banners or flags larger than 15 square feet throughout the park. The ban took effect on the date of Joslin's display.\par \par \uc2 Joslin's lawyers say no punishment was given to other, similar flag bearers. They said that with Joslin's firing, the government violated the First Amendment by engaging in viewpoint discrimination.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has taken steps to remove mentions of trans people and gender identity from federal websites and public displays. The trans flag was a target, according to Joslin, because "it expresses an identity that has then been turned into an ideology that this administration wants to villainize."\par \par \uc2 At the time of Joslin's termination, an Interior Department spokeswoman said the Park Service and Justice Department were pursuing administrative action against several Yosemite employees. She said charges were being explored against visitors who allegedly violated federal laws related to demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 She also said at the time of Joslin's firing that displaying signs, banners and flags outside designated areas "detracts from visitor experience and the protection of the park."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260224em2o0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
