\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- AI, Tech Targeted As Trade-Secrets Thefts Increase\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amrith Ramkumar and Katherine Blunt \par \uc2 730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The indictment of a former \uc2 Google\uc2  employee accused of stealing trade secrets related to the company's Pixel phones, adds to cases highlighting the security threats companies face in an era of rapidly evolving technology.\par \par \uc2 The employee, who worked as a \uc2 Google\uc2  hardware engineer from 2019 to 2023, was charged Thursday with six theft-related charges. Her husband and sister were indicted on similar charges after allegedly working with her to steal trade secrets from \uc2 Google\uc2  and two other tech companies. The defendants, from Iran, had pursued advanced degrees in the U.S. and careers in tech.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Google\uc2  case is the third high-profile incident in recent years involving misappropriation of the company's trade secrets. A former software engineer was recently convicted by a federal jury of stealing information about the company's artificial-intelligence chips in 2022 and 2023 to benefit China. The Justice Department said that case marked the first conviction on economic-espionage charges related to AI. In late 2024, a judge mandated that a former engineer stop posting photos of internal documents about coming Pixel chips.\par \par \uc2 The incidents show the value of sensitive information needed to make the chips and devices powering the modern economy. Secrets behind critical technology can be worth billions of dollars, making them an attractive target. AI is adding to the challenge by making trade secrets more lucrative and giving thieves a new tool to deploy in their heists.\par \par \uc2 In response, companies are tightening their security protocols, changes that challenge the open corporate culture many Silicon Valley companies prize.\par \par \uc2 "Our threat model is changing in such a way that we're going to move more in real time and move the bar a little further," Heather Adkins, \uc2 Google\uc2 's vice president of security engineering, said in an interview. "Those open environments will become more constrained." The company said its controls have allowed thieves to be caught and brought to justice.\par \par \uc2 Other tech companies face similar challenges. \uc2 Apple\uc2  this past summer sued former engineers who are accused of taking trade secrets about the \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch and Vision Pro headset. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 xAI\uc2  is suing a former engineer who allegedly stole secrets about its Grok chatbot before selling millions of dollars of stock and joining a competitor.\par \par \uc2 There were around 1,500 federal trade-secrets cases filed in the U.S. last year, up 20% from the previous year and the highest figure in at least a decade, according to the data provider \uc2 Lex Machina\uc2 . "It seems to be getting worse in terms of the economic impact," said Justin Sherman, chief executive of Global Cyber Strategies, a research and advisory firm.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 , whose parent is \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , has a range of security controls at its disposal, including restricting badge access for employees, requiring computers to be used for certain purposes, and making engineers do their most sensitive work in rooms where outside computers and phones aren't allowed. This past summer, \uc2 Google\uc2  said in an email to employees that it was restricting the use of third-party applications and AI systems.\par \par \uc2 "Many people would imagine it couldn't happen at \uc2 Google\uc2 ," said Lee-Anne Mulholland, the company's global head of regulatory affairs. "We come at it from the perspective that anything can happen, so we have to protect from it before it does."\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  uses tools to limit who has access to sensitive information, the company said in a lawsuit alleging that a former engineer stole \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch information to take to a Chinese competitor. Around eight years ago, a former \uc2 Apple\uc2  employee was apprehended by federal agents just before boarding a flight to Beijing with secrets tied to the company's autonomous-driving program.\par \par \uc2 Linwei Ding, the former \uc2 Google\uc2  employee recently convicted of stealing sensitive information, had booked a flight to return to China when \uc2 Google\uc2  notified authorities so they could charge him before he left. Ding downloaded documents to his personal \uc2 Google\uc2  account and founded his own tech company in China to use the information.\par \par \uc2 Another \uc2 Google\uc2  employee used Ding's badge to come to the office so it looked as though he was still in the U.S. when he had spent months in China. Ding had pleaded not guilty. He intends to file a motion for a new trial, his lawyer said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich466\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6990
