\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: L.A. Is Next Target for California Teachers --- emboldened by statewide gains, union members back strike option\b0\par\par\uc2 By Douglas Belkin And Scott Calvert \par \uc2 952 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last week's teacher strike in San Francisco was a test of a statewide union push that is raising tensions in school districts across California. The next big target: Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 A wave of teacher contracts is up for renegotiation now, thanks to a strategy the unions implemented a few years ago to synchronize expiration dates. Dozens of California school districts are in talks, with some already at an impasse or in mediation.\par \par \uc2 Teachers in Los Angeles, the country's second-largest school district, have voted to authorize a strike, empowering union leaders to call for a walkout if a deal isn't made.\par \par \uc2 By coordinating negotiations with strike threats, the unions aim to push funding battles from local school boards to the state legislature, said David Goldberg, president of the \uc2 California Teachers Association\uc2 , a union representing 300,000 educators. "This resets the power dynamic," Goldberg said.\par \par \uc2 Teachers want higher wages and better healthcare benefits as the cost of living has jumped and student behavior has deteriorated since Covid. School districts say they are strapped.\par \par \uc2 In San Francisco, school and union officials agreed to a tentative contract last week after a four-day strike, the first there in nearly 50 years. The district agreed to an effective 5% pay raise over two years and fully funded healthcare benefits starting in 2027. The district says the benefits will cost more than $40 million a year.\par \par \uc2 Enrollment at San Francisco Unified has been declining for decades and is projected to drop by about 12% by 2032, according to the district. Critics says the district has too many schools, teachers and staff.\par \par \uc2 The district faces a $100 million deficit, and has cut hundreds of central-office jobs and enacted early-retirement incentives. It pays the equivalent of 19% of teacher salaries to the state pension fund.\par \par \uc2 "We stretched our resources to the limit to get this agreement done. We still have a long way ahead of us where difficult choices remain," San Francisco Superintendent Maria Su said. She didn't rule out layoffs or school closures, which she said have "been on the table."\par \par \uc2 The union says the district can afford the proposed contract, which needs approval from both sides, and notes its leaders bargained for 11 months before calling a strike.\par \par \uc2 "If other school districts and unions are at a position of stalemate, I would hope that what just happened in San Francisco would be an inspiration," said Cassondra Curiel, president of United Educators of San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 In Los Angeles, the union and district have been negotiating for about a year toward a new contract. The prior pact expired last summer.\par \par \uc2 Teachers want an immediate 16% jump in starting teacher pay, to $80,000, with smaller increases for more experienced teachers, along with a 3% raise in the contract's second year. The district has countered with a 4.5% raise over two years, plus a one-time 1% bonus.\par \par \uc2 Total enrollment in the district has fallen by about 20% since 2018-19 to just over 390,000 students, excluding independent charter schools, officials say. Over that span, the number of teachers has held at 25,500 while overall staffing has increased 15%.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 Los Angeles Unified\uc2  school board approved as many as 657 layoffs, concentrated in the district's central office. Superintendent \uc2 Alberto Carvalho\uc2  cited enrollment declines and the Covid-era hiring of 6,000 employees using cash infusions.\par \par \uc2 "At some point, we reach the breaking point," he said.\par \par \uc2 The San Francisco strike sent an encouraging message to other educators, said \uc2 Gloria Martinez\uc2 , treasurer of the Los Angeles teachers union. "Putting pressure on the district is always going to turn up additional funds," she said. "I'm hoping the [Los Angeles] district is paying attention to what happened."\par \par \uc2 Al Muratsuchi, who heads the California Assembly's education committee, has twice introduced bills to lift teacher salaries by 50% at a cost exceeding $30 billion a year. He hopes the coordinated campaign will provide the political clout to pass the bill. "These rolling teacher strikes have certainly elevated the awareness of the challenges our educators face," he said.\par \par \uc2 The coordinated campaign in California takes a page from organized labor in other industries, including autos and hotels, said Rebecca Givan, an associate professor of labor studies and employment relations at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  in New Jersey. The joint efforts increase leverage for workers by improving messaging and strengthening solidarity.\par \par \uc2 "Teachers and workers are more willing to strike because they are seeing other successful strikes and that makes them feel like they could win something," Givan said.\par \par \uc2 In Sacramento, teachers in the Natomas School District this month authorized a work stoppage after a year of failed negotiations. Their union joined the statewide campaign because they saw many of the problems they were fighting were shared by other districts, including rising housing prices and healthcare costs, said Nico Vaccaro, president of the Natomas Teachers' Association.\par \par \uc2 "There are so many stories of teachers working two or three jobs because they are trying to figure out how to pay for their healthcare and how they are going to make ends meet," Vaccaro said.\par \par \uc2 Robyn Castillo, Natomas's superintendent, is hopeful the state will direct more money to local districts but condemned the union's campaign.\par \par \uc2 "We're very disappointed to see the CTA statewide strategy," she said. "It seems to be disrupting the collaborative, normally locally-focused bargaining that's always been part of our labor relations here in Natomas and our students should not be used to make a political statement."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260221em2l00014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
