\par\par \b \uc2 Workplace Technology (A Special Report) --- A Job-Interview Video Glitch Is Harmless, Right? Hardly: When the internet goes even slightly awry, the impact on your professional life could be huge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacqueline Rifkin and Melanie Brucks \par \uc2 800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 We all know the feeling. You're on \uc2 Zoom\uc2 , meeting with colleagues or catching up with a friend or trying to nail a job interview, when it happens: a freeze, garbled audio, a blank screen.\par \par \uc2 In a world where more interactions are happening on video calls, glitches have become a normal, everyday occurrence. And sure, glitches happen, but they are no big deal, right? At least that's what \uc2 Zoom\uc2 -reliant professionals and casual users told us: Glitches are annoying but harmless.\par \par \uc2 But, as consumer psychologists who study the nuanced and often-overlooked factors that influence everyday judgments and decisions, we weren't so sure. Might these brief audiovisual interruptions have an unexpected negative effect on our professional and personal lives?\par \par \uc2 We, along with our co-author, Jeff Johnson of the University of Missouri-Kansas City, conducted a series of experiments putting glitches to the test.\par \par \uc2 In one experiment, we showed over 3,000 people a prerecorded job interview conducted via video call. They either saw the recording with no interruptions, or interspersed with one of 10 different glitch types, including freezes, lags, blank screens, pixelations and echoes. We then asked people how they'd feel about hiring this candidate as an in-person member of their team.\par \par \uc2 Compared with people who experienced no glitches, those who saw a glitchy video were significantly less supportive of hiring the candidate. We found this same pattern in many other domains: Glitches made it less likely somebody would "like" a potential new friend after a "get to know you" video chat, trust a medical provider's recommendation after a telehealth call, and be willing to hire a financial adviser after a virtual sales pitch. We found this pattern even when people didn't blame the other person for the glitchy connection, and when they were able to fully understand each other.\par \par \uc2 Many different types of glitches damaged hiring evaluations, but some were worse than others. For example, whereas a distorted video or lag had a negligible impact, glitches involving sustained video loss, echoes or brief freezes were substantially more detrimental to the candidate's likelihood of being hired. Not surprisingly, audio dropouts were the most problematic -- after all, if you can't hear what someone has to say, it's hard to support their candidacy.\par \par \uc2 To understand why glitches are so bad, think about why video calling is so good. If you can't interact with someone in person, video calling is the next best substitute. You can hear, see and react to each other in real time. It feels like you're sitting together.\par \par \uc2 But when there's a glitch, this mirage breaks. Real-life human faces don't freeze, and voices don't echo. As a result, when glitches occur, things start to feel off -- creepy or strange -- and this unsettling feeling is what's responsible for the ensuing negative judgments. Look no further than iconic horror films like "Cloverfield" or "The Ring," where glitches signify the presence of something unnatural and malicious.\par \par \uc2 The impact of glitches are especially pernicious for people on the less-privileged side of the digital divide. Those with lower socioeconomic status, from more rural communities, or with unstable housing are likely to miss out on opportunities stemming from their systematically worse internet connection (and glitchier calls). This means that virtual communication is a double-edged sword. Although the internet is meant to be an equalizer, unreliable or inconsistent connectivity can instead hold people back.\par \par \uc2 Our research uncovered one particularly sobering example. In an analysis of six months' worth of online parole hearings, we found that the likelihood of an inmate receiving parole -- an outcome that is completely unrelated to the court's \uc2 Zoom\uc2  connection -- was 12 percentage points lower when the hearing was glitchy.\par \par \uc2 One obvious takeaway is that if you have an important meeting, and if resources allow, it might be worth the trip to meet in person. If not, ensure you have a strong and reliable connection.\par \par \uc2 But we recognize that people don't always have this kind of access or flexibility. We need to consider how to make internet resources more equitable: For instance, employers could offer job candidates a place with reliable internet to conduct interviews or spring for high-quality internet for remote workers. Otherwise, glitches could potentially cost you a job, a friendship or your freedom.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jacqueline Rifkin is an assistant professor of marketing at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 's SC Johnson College of Business. Melanie Brucks is an assistant professor of marketing at \uc2 Columbia Business School\uc2 . They can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260220em2k00004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
