\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's Southern Strategy\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  President Shawn Fain is celebrating the union's new collective-bargaining agreement at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's plant in Chattanooga, Tenn., as he tries to build momentum to organize foreign auto makers. The deal is a big win for the union, but it's a big gamble for workers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has sought to organize foreign-owned auto plants in the South for decades with little success. But it notched a major victory two years ago in Chattanooga, despite workers there having previously rejected the union twice. Mr. Fain prevailed by advertising the rich contracts the \uc2 UAW\uc2  had recently reached with Detroit's Big Three car makers. VW had also agreed not to oppose the union, perhaps to curry favor with its Biden overlords doling out electric-vehicle subsidies.\par \par \uc2 Chattanooga workers this week will vote on what Mr. Fain calls "a life-changing first agreement." There's no question the deal does look sweet in the short-term. It includes a 20% wage increase over four years, a $4,000 ratification bonus and $2,550 annual lump-sum payments. The deal begins "to close the gap with industry standards," the \uc2 UAW\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 This is an acknowledgment that the deal's wages and benefits fall short of what other auto workers receive. The top rate will rise to $34 an hour, which is less than the roughly $39 an hour that the highest-paid \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and non-unionized Mercedes workers make. In late 2023, Hyundai said it would raise its top wage by 33% by 2028 to about $42 an hour.\par \par \uc2 While the deal's bonuses and lump-sum payments are sizable, they fall far short of the profit-sharing that workers at Detroit's Big Three, Mercedes and some other foreign auto plants receive. The Mercedes comparison is especially relevant, since Mr. Fain is set on organizing its Alabama plant, where workers rejected the union in 2024.\par \par \uc2 His goal is to talk up the VW agreement to cajole workers at Mercedes and other foreign plants into unionizing. But the biggest winner in Chattanooga is the union. VW agreed to pay 34 employees to work full-time as union representatives at the plant, rather than on making vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Workers will have to pay a minimum of 1.4% of their paychecks in dues. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  can use this money to fund its organizing campaigns and left-wing advocacy, including on college campuses. Academic workers including graduate students make up about a quarter of \uc2 UAW\uc2 's members. The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's college chapters helped spearhead anti-Israel protests.\par \par \uc2 A big risk for Chattanooga workers is that the 250-page deal could make \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  less efficient and competitive. The deal would let the union micromanage work at the plant. It gives the union a say in "equipment and process design development, modification, and repurposing" and requires breaks at least every two and a half hours. It also establishes a company-funded Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Committee to ensure "inclusive behaviors."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has lost more than a million members since its heyday in the 1970s because its labor agreements made Detroit less competitive vis-a-vis foreign manufacturers. Some workers at the Chattanooga plant are seeking to decertify the union. Maybe they realize their jobs depend on VW's success, not Mr. Fain's.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260218em2i00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
