\par\par \b \uc2 Midwestern Ad Agencies See an Opportunity\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Deighton \par \uc2 760 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 3,000 American advertising jobs were eliminated in December. None was at the Bark Firm in Casper, Wyo.\par \par \uc2 The 17-year-old agency has grown its head count steadily from two people to 10. Clients come and go but business is predictable, largely because of a strategy to service mostly midsize, Midwestern accounts -- never pitching for work that would require staffing up dramatically and potentially making layoffs later.\par \par \uc2 "In middle America, some brands and businesses are more afraid to trust their budgets with these larger [ad] firms where the use of technology is rampant, and they don't have that human connection," said Dustin Neal, partner and principal creative at the firm, whose clients include coffee brand Sofa King and logistics company Takkion, both based nearby. "They want to really know the people who are running with their budget."\par \par \uc2 Agencies like Bark are emerging as one of the bright spots in the advertising business, for both employees and clients, as changes around them recast slow growth as stability and a Midwestern sensibility as a strength. It helps that their rates don't need to cover the cost of Manhattan real estate or salaries, they say.\par \par \uc2 The prototypical agency holding company that long lured in staff with skyscraper offices, cushy expense accounts and multimillion-dollar clients is in disarray. Last year's merger of \uc2 Interpublic Group of Cos.\uc2  and \uc2 Omnicom Group\uc2  saw 4,000 jobs slashed and decades-old agency brands like \uc2 FCB\uc2  and \uc2 DDB\uc2  eradicated. The market capitalization of Ogilvy parent \uc2 WPP\uc2 , once the largest advertising company in the world, has fallen from more than $30 billion in 2017 to less than $4 billion today. Even \uc2 Publicis Groupe\uc2 , which posted organic net revenue growth of 5.9% for the quarter ended Dec. 31, saw its shares fall sharply in February after its outlook failed to dispel investor concerns about the impact of artificial intelligence on its business.\par \par \uc2 The old Mad Men model of long client lunches and taglines that lasted decades has been worn down by the brisk pace and constant measurement of digital media. Now clients ask their agencies: Can AI help you do the work even faster, and for less money?\par \par \uc2 Some noncoastal ad firms as a result are more aggressively pitching themselves as the friendly antithesis to faceless, famous agencies, now embracing rather than apologizing for their lack of metropolitan elitism.\par \par \uc2 "In 2013, I had to fight the battle of, 'You're not in New York or Chicago,'" said \uc2 Ken Luce\uc2 , the president of ad agency LDWW, which has offices in Dallas and Oklahoma City. "I don't fight that battle anymore."\par \par \uc2 Large agencies have long grappled with tension between client briefs to reach swaths of the U.S. population and staff that tends to be made up of white, college-educated professionals who don't spend much time outside of big, blue cities. The disconnect was highlighted by their surprise at the strength of Donald Trump's first presidential campaign, despite their avowed prowess with "consumer insights."\par \par \uc2 Such agencies have occasionally tried to correct for their cultural blinders. The award-winning, New York-based agency \uc2 Droga5\uc2  in the spring of 2016 introduced an ad campaign for the sausage brand Johnsonville that it said was created by flying a cohort of its employees to Sheboygan Falls, Wis., to interview workers at the client.\par \par \uc2 Ten years on, Johnsonville is instead working with Hold Fast, an ad agency based closer to home in Milwaukee that was hired in 2020. \uc2 Droga5\uc2 , which was bought by \uc2 Accenture\uc2 's Song creative unit in 2019, said it continues to work with clients across the country but now makes greater use of technology to conduct qualitative research with consumers in a faster and more cost-effective manner. Its talent base is representative of the diversity of the American consumer, said Michael Osbourn, \uc2 Droga5\uc2 's head of strategy.\par \par \uc2 Not every midsize agency in the Midwest is thriving, and not every large holding company is struggling, said Mollie Rosen, president of member experience for advertising agency trade group the 4As. Large multinational companies still require the manpower of big, connected agencies with offices in multiple markets, and the vast majority of Super Bowl ads this month were created by large agencies based in New York, Chicago or Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 But smaller agencies across the board are getting better at refining their own brands and hiring employees jumping ship or laid off from big competitors, Rosen said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Katie Deighton writes for WSJ Leadership Institute's CMO Today.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260218em2i00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
