\par\par \b \uc2 Smithfield Plans to Build Its First New Pork Plant in Decades\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Thomas \par \uc2 611 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Smithfield Foods\uc2  plans to build a large hog slaughterhouse in South Dakota, marking the first new facility built by America's top pork producer in decades.\par \par \uc2 Virginia-based Smithfield said it will spend about $1.3 billion over three years on a new facility in Sioux Falls, S.D. When in operation, the plant is expected to employ about 3,000 workers and be able to slaughter about 20,000 hogs a day. The new project replaces Smithfield's existing, more than 100-year-old facility in the city.\par \par \uc2 The company slaughters nearly 30 million hogs a year and is a major supplier of ham, bacon and other pork products to grocers. Its plans come a year after its return to U.S. public markets. Smithfield's stock is up nearly 20% since the listing.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. [meat] industry in general is older and dated," Smithfield Chief Executive Officer \uc2 Shane Smith\uc2  said in an interview. "I think this sends a signal that we are in the pork industry for the long term. We're not going anywhere."\par \par \uc2 The project could face scrutiny from U.S. officials concerned about foreign ownership of meat production and agricultural land.\par \par \uc2 Since 2013, Smithfield has been a subsidiary of Chinese pork company \uc2 WH Group\uc2 . At that time, the $4.7 billion deal was one of the largest takeovers of a U.S. company by a Chinese firm. The ownership of Smithfield and its U.S. assets has drawn scrutiny from lawmakers and the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The company has said it is investing in the U.S. and denied accusations that its decisions are influenced by the Chinese government.\par \par \uc2 "Do I expect to hear some noise? Probably. But do I think it should rise to a level where it's an issue? I don't think so," Smith said. "Especially, again, if people look at this objectively."\par \par \uc2 Founded in 1936, Smithfield helped pioneer the methods that made the U.S. a global pork powerhouse.\par \par \uc2 The new South Dakota plant will expand its packaged meats production -- a priority for Smithfield's CEO. It would be the second-largest for Smithfield in the U.S., behind its Tar Heel, N.C., facility -- the world's largest pork processing facility by volume.\par \par \uc2 Construction of a new meatpacking facility would follow several recent plant closures. \uc2 Tyson Foods\uc2  closed one of its largest beef processing plants, in Lexington, Neb., which employed 3,200 people, and cut production at a Texas facility in half. JBS and Cargill have said they are closing smaller processing facilities in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 New meatpacking plants at the scale Smithfield is planning are rare. Besides being costly, the projects have historically faced criticism from local residents and environmentalists.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, JBS, the world's largest meatpacker and Smithfield's top pork rival, has said it is expanding its pork operations in response to Americans' growing appetite for protein. The company has announced plans to build two new sausage facilities in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Smithfield also has closed facilities over the past few years, including a plant in California and smaller processing operations that packaged meat for grocery stores.\par \par \uc2 The company said the new facility will run more efficiently than other plants, featuring advanced automation technology and a streamlined design. Smithfield and other meatpackers have been spending billions of dollars to automate the grueling, sometimes dangerous work required in meat-processing facilities.\par \par \uc2 Work on the site is expected to begin this spring. Smithfield plans to formally break ground on construction in the first half of 2027 and begin production at the end of 2028.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260217em2h0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
