\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Blowout Month, Revisions Show Shifting Workforce\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman, Danny Dougherty and Justin Lahart \par \uc2 506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The American workforce is increasingly donning scrubs.\par \par \uc2 January's strong jobs report, and sweeping revisions that slashed jobs numbers for the past two years, show how much the U.S. labor market is transforming. The mixed jobs picture reflects surging demand for people in healthcare and social services but a bleaker environment for government employees, factory workers and cubicle dwellers.\par \par \uc2 Losers and winners\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department's broad revisions lowered previous estimates of how many jobs the economy added in the past two calendar years, spanning the end of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's term and President Trump's first year back in office. The revisions took an especially big bite out of previous tallies for construction, manufacturing, and retail jobs in 2025.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare\par \par \uc2 Hiring in the healthcare sector is the big story of the U.S. labor market. The industry added about 437,000 jobs in January from a year earlier, while the closely allied social-assistance sector -- which includes jobs such as home health aides -- added about 321,000.\par \par \uc2 These roles buoyed the job market over the past 12 months on their own; all other private-sector jobs combined have shrunk.\par \par \uc2 Empty cubicles\par \par \uc2 The past year was lackluster for many white-collar workers. The financial-activities sector, which includes banking and insurance, in January had about 25,000 fewer jobs than a year earlier, and employment in the field has been almost entirely unchanged since the start of 2024. The professional and business-services sector fared worse, driven by a large decline in employment at temporary staffing agencies -- though it has been rebounding in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Economic uncertainty has dissuaded many of these employers from hiring. And a growing share say they are experimenting with the use of AI to replace some functions.\par \par \uc2 Blue-collar blues\par \par \uc2 President Trump cruised back into office on promises of reviving the manufacturing sector. But January was the first month of his second term where employment in the sector actually expanded, adding a modest 5,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 On the whole, manufacturing employment has endured a steady multiyear decline.\par \par \uc2 Data centers\par \par \uc2 Construction has remained sluggish, held back by last year's elevated interest rates and economic uncertainty that continued to weigh on the housing market. Yet overall construction employment has grown in recent months, driven especially by gains among nonresidential specialty trade contractors.\par \par \uc2 Those jobs represent strong demand for the sort of workers assembling data centers to power artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Immigration impact\par \par \uc2 Months of slow corporate hiring have been hard on job seekers struggling to find their next gig. It is one factor behind consistently lackluster consumer-sentiment readings.\par \par \uc2 The slow-growing job market also reflects tougher immigration enforcement, which has drastically reduced the supply of new workers. As a result, economists believe the U.S. needs to add far fewer jobs than in the past to keep unemployment from rising -- one reason it has held a low level, like January's 4.3%.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw537\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8055\pichgoal7200
