\par\par \b \uc2 Hiring Rebounded in January --- Health sector drives a surge of 130,000 jobs while revisions sharply cut '25 totals\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 933 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy added 130,000 jobs in January, its strongest growth in more than a year, in a sign that the labor market might be shaking off its recent stagnation.\par \par \uc2 January's robust gains after weak job growth in 2025 surprised forecasters, blowing far past consensus expectations while solidifying expectations the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will keep rates on hold for the foreseeable future. The unemployment rate dipped to 4.3% from 4.4% in December, the Labor Department said Wednesday. And workers' wages rose.\par \par \uc2 Revisions to nearly two years of jobs figures sharply downgraded the earlier reports, particularly for 2025, which saw a paltry 181,000 jobs added for the entire year.\par \par \uc2 The January gains were highly concentrated in healthcare and social-assistance fields, which includes jobs like home health aides and residential care workers. Such jobs have long been an engine of U.S. job growth.\par \par \uc2 The picture was much more mixed for white-collar workers. Professional and business service firms added tens of thousands of jobs, but 34,000 jobs were lost across the financial activities and information sectors.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, manufacturing also added workers for the first time in more than a year. And the construction sector grew by 33,000 jobs, mostly on the back of expanding appetite for new data centers.\par \par \uc2 "It does look like the labor market is beginning to heal from the trauma we saw it go through last year," said \uc2 Diane Swonk\uc2 , chief economist for \uc2 KPMG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Trump's tariff announcements in 2025 caused companies to put plans on hold, contributing to the weak hiring environment, Swonk said. Now, many of the chief executives she speaks with "are still unclear about what policies will look like this year -- but they're moving forward after shelving many projects."\par \par \uc2 The January numbers improved on the 48,000 jobs added in December, according to the Labor Department, while surging beyond the 55,000 expected by analysts polled by The Wall Street Journal. The monthly figures are seasonally adjusted.\par \par \uc2 The strongest job growth since December 2024 also marks a turnaround after hiring slowed markedly in 2025, especially after new revisions from the Labor Department show just how tough the market has been for job seekers.\par \par \uc2 The previously scheduled revisions sharply cut prior job numbers. The net result: the U.S. produced 1.5 million jobs in 2024, versus the previously estimated 2 million. The 181,000 jobs last year were down from the previously estimated 584,000.\par \par \uc2 Stocks edged lower, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average falling nearly 67 points, or 0.1%, ending a streak of three straight records. The S&P 500 was just about flat on the day, while the Nasdaq Composite fell by 36 points.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Simons, chief U.S. economist at Jefferies, said that January's high concentration of healthcare jobs "should temper enthusiasm quite a bit" for the report, and that there were other signs that gave him pause. "It's hard to get terribly excited when you see pretty significant declines in financial services and trade, transportation and utilities -- the kind of stuff you think of as being relatively high-paying jobs."\par \par \uc2 Trade, transportation and utilities shed a combined 9,000 jobs in January.\par \par \uc2 The January report is likely to cement the Fed's wait-and-see posture and makes it difficult for officials to build a case for further rate cuts on the basis of labor-market weakness. The Fed at its latest meeting in late January held interest rates steady after three consecutive cuts.\par \par \uc2 The new numbers could give more ammunition to inflation-wary "hawks" who will point to signs that interest rates aren't meaningfully restricting economic activity. That evidence includes the continued decline in the jobless rate, a drop in the number of people working part-time jobs because they couldn't find full-time work, and a decrease in ranks of those unemployed for more than six months.\par \par \uc2 Among other positive signs, the median length of unemployment last month declined to 11.1 weeks, down from 11.4 weeks in December. Average hourly and weekly earnings ticked up. At the same time, though, the unemployment rate for people who held at least a bachelor's degree increased slightly to 2.9%.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. job market has been defined for months by companies holding off on new hires, but not slashing jobs en masse. The situation has made it harder for young graduates to get a foothold and left many unemployed people stuck in long, fruitless job searches.\par \par \uc2 Another factor that hit 2025 jobs data: the Trump administration's cuts to the federal workforce through a combination of layoffs and voluntary buyouts.\par \par \uc2 The White House trumpeted the jobs report as a sign that "President Trump's economic agenda continues to pay off," according to a statement by deputy press secretary Kush Desai. "The unemployment rate fell and private sector job growth remains robust," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Brendan Boyle (D., Pa.), ranking member of the House Budget Committee, said in his own statement that the report "doesn't change the fact that costs are out of control, the labor market is softening, and Trump's tariffs amount to a tax hike on American families."\par \par \uc2 The mood among U.S. consumers has improved slightly, according to the University of Michigan's index of consumer sentiment. Nonetheless, at 57.3, its preliminary February reading remains substantially lower than the 64.7 it clocked a year earlier.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich310\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4650
