\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Healthcare Is Propping Up The Job Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Spencer Jakab \par \uc2 428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy might be in the hospital. Literally.\par \par \uc2 Amid all the hand-wringing about a decline in job openings and rising layoffs, there's a less-followed labor-market trend that's been a saving grace.\par \par \uc2 It's worth watching when the Labor Department releases delayed January data on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare and social-assistance jobs have boomed, even as most others haven't. Year-over-year payroll growth outside of that area turned negative in December for the first time since the last recession. In other words, all of America's jobs growth now comes from a category responsible for less than 15% of total employment.\par \par \uc2 What happens if healthcare employers need to slow down? It's a valid concern given cutbacks in government spending on Medicaid and insurers retreating from that even bigger government program, Medicare.\par \par \uc2 The boom in health jobs isn't recent, but it has become more noticeable as other sectors got cautious. In the past three years, health and social-assistance employment grew by 12.5% while growth in every other job category has been less than 2%.\par \par \uc2 The demand for more labor can be explained in part by demographics: Americans are getting older. A record number turned 65 last year -- an age when health and elder care needs go up and Medicare coverage kicks in.\par \par \uc2 But every other developed country faces similar strains, and U.S. health spending per person is an outlier -- twice as high as in Britain or Japan, for example. Look at the top single employer in each U.S. state; in many it's a healthcare company, with only \uc2 Walmart\uc2  being the top employer in more places.\par \par \uc2 So what happens if cutbacks by insurers or by the government actually result in less hiring? We're in an unusual moment when AI infrastructure spending is by far the most important driver of U.S. economic growth.\par \par \uc2 That could create an unusual situation in which overall employment declines but the economy keeps expanding on the back of things like data centers and chips that don't require as many workers -- jobless growth.\par \par \uc2 No matter how rich some people get by investing in artificial intelligence, the vast majority depend on wage income. Fewer people getting paychecks squeezes consumer spending and also federal and state tax revenue.\par \par \uc2 High healthcare costs certainly are a drag on U.S. competitiveness. Trying to solve that with a sharp cutback in government spending could spark some nasty withdrawal symptoms.\par \par \uc2 page,5043 \par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260211em2b0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
