\par\par \b \uc2 Washington Post Publisher Lewis Exits --- Move follows steep layoffs last week; D'Onofrio tapped as acting publisher, CEO\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Korn and Alexandra Bruell \par \uc2 713 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 's publisher, \uc2 Will Lewis\uc2 , is leaving his role days after the company announced it was laying off one-third of its staff.\par \par \uc2 The publication named finance chief \uc2 Jeff D'Onofrio\uc2 , who joined in June, as its new acting publisher and CEO, effective immediately.\par \par \uc2 Lewis is departing after two tumultuous years atop the organization. He arrived vowing to carry out a digital transformation, stem financial losses and reverse a decline in online readership.\par \par \uc2 Lewis, who was previously CEO of Dow Jones, The Wall Street Journal's parent company, laid out a plan to build a "third newsroom" at the Post for social media and service journalism, apart from its core news operation and its opinion section. But the publication struggled to find its footing and has since revised that plan.\par \par \uc2 Lewis said in a note to staff Saturday that, "after two years of transformation at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , now is the right time for me to step aside."\par \par \uc2 "During my tenure, difficult decisions have been taken in order to ensure the sustainable future of The Post," he wrote. Lewis didn't immediately respond to a request for comment Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 Many publications large and small are grappling with shifting reader habits, a decline in traffic from tech giants and generative AI tools that are changing the way people search for and consume information online.\par \par \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  staffers criticized Lewis -- and Post owner Jeff Bezos -- for not being involved in communicating last week's job cuts. Journalists have said publicly that Lewis didn't engage with the newsroom regularly for most of his tenure at the paper.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has been battered around for a number of years, and we've had a lot of challenges," Executive Editor \uc2 Matt Murray\uc2  said on Puck's "The Grill Room" podcast Friday. He said there were cost and structure issues that had built up over years that were "more deeply problematic" than he and Lewis knew.\par \par \uc2 Before becoming an executive, Lewis was a reporter and editor, including at the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 . He joined \uc2 News Corp\uc2 's business side in 2010 and, during his time as CEO, increased the Journal's digital subscribers. In 2020, he co-founded a media startup, the News Movement, that made content for \uc2 TikTok\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lewis became CEO and publisher of the Post in early 2024, soon after Bezos told the newsroom that he wanted the paper to return to profitability.\par \par \uc2 He said the British executive's background in award-winning journalism made him the right choice to lead the paper.\par \par \uc2 Lewis was dogged later that year -- as legal proceedings played out in the U.K. -- by criticism of his work for \uc2 News Corp\uc2  more than a decade earlier during fallout from a phone-hacking scandal.\par \par \uc2 The Post lost a wave of subscribers in late 2024 after announcing it wouldn't be endorsing any candidate for that year's presidential election. Early last year, it narrowed its opinion section to focus on the support and defense of "personal liberties and free markets."\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Post said it would close its sports department in its current form, restructure its metro section, eliminate its books section and pare back its international coverage, among other changes.\par \par \uc2 It now plans to focus primarily on national news and features, investigations and advice on health and wellness topics.\par \par \uc2 Murray, who was previously editor in chief of the Journal, said the cuts will allow the company to be more flexible and provide readers with more relevant content.\par \par \uc2 "Each and every day our readers give us a road map to success. The data tells us what is valuable and where to focus," Bezos said in a statement Saturday announcing D'Onofrio's appointment.\par \par \uc2 D'Onofrio, who was previously finance chief at digital ad company Raptive and CEO of \uc2 Tumblr\uc2 , said in a letter to Post staff Saturday that he looked forward to leading the paper "into a sustainable, successful future with the strength of our journalism as our north star."\par \par \uc2 He said decisions would be driven by customer data, "sharpening our edge in delivering what is most valuable to our audiences."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260209em290001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
