\par\par \b \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  Cuts One-Third Of Staff as It Retools\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexandra Bruell \par \uc2 447 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is cutting one-third of its staff, slashing hundreds of jobs across the newsroom and other departments in an effort to trim costs and reshape coverage.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs will affect journalists in nearly all news departments, including the sports, foreign, technology and breaking-news teams, as well as business and technology staff.\par \par \uc2 "If we are to thrive, not just endure, we must reinvent our journalism and our business model with renewed ambition," Executive Editor \uc2 Matt Murray\uc2  wrote in a note to newsroom staff Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Post and many other publications have struggled to find a sustainable business model as readers' news-consumption habits change and web traffic wanes. Murray said the restructuring is intended to make the Post more flexible and provide audiences with more relevant content.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Murray declined to say how many of the cuts were coming from the newsroom.\par \par \uc2 The Post is closing its sports department but will retain some roles in that coverage area. It is shrinking its international coverage, and will focus on national news and features, investigations and advice on health and wellness topics.\par \par \uc2 Murray said the Post will continue to have correspondents in about a dozen international locations, and will focus on issues related to national security. "That's what our readers seem to respond to the most," he said. The Post also will restructure its metro section, close its Books section and suspend its Post Reports podcast.\par \par \uc2 Murray said in his note to staff that the Post's video department hasn't kept up with changes in how consumers get their information, and noted the publication's daily story count has fallen in recent years. "And even as we produce much excellent work, we too often write from one perspective, for one slice of the audience," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Post lost $77 million in 2023 and $100 million in 2024, The Wall Street Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 The company said Wednesday that organic search traffic has fallen by nearly half in the past three years. Publisher \uc2 William Lewis\uc2  aims to have the Post break even by the end of 2026, according to a Post spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 The paper's owner, Jeff Bezos, tapped Lewis, former chief executive of Journal publisher Dow Jones, in late 2023 to spearhead a turnaround. Murray, a former Journal editor in chief, joined in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The Post has conducted multiple rounds of buyouts and layoffs in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The paper faced immense backlash in 2024 after Bezos pulled a prewritten endorsement of then-Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  just before the presidential election. Within days, it lost more than 250,000 subscriptions.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260205em250001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
