\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Manufacturing Is in Retreat --- Factories cut jobs as president's promised boom from tariffs hasn't occurred\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Uberti \par \uc2 1,074 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The manufacturing boom President Trump promised would usher in a golden age for the U.S. is going in reverse. After years of economic interventions by the Trump and Biden administrations, fewer people work in manufacturing than any point since the pandemic ended.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturers shed workers in each of the eight months after Trump unveiled "Liberation Day" tariffs, according to federal figures, extending a contraction that has seen more than 200,000 roles disappear since 2023.\par \par \uc2 An index of factory activity tracked by the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2  shrunk in 26 straight months through December, but showed a January uptick in new orders and production that surprised analysts. The Census Bureau estimated that manufacturing construction spending, which surged with Biden-era funding for chips and renewable energy, fell in each of Trump's first nine months in office.\par \par \uc2 The gradual slowdown is in some ways a continuation of decadeslong trends that pulled factory jobs overseas and helped empty out Midwestern cities. In an industry where capital plans and construction timelines extend years into the future, turnarounds also don't happen overnight.\par \par \uc2 In November, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  slashed estimates for overall U.S. output since the pandemic in an annual revision to metrics for industrial production.\par \par \uc2 "We never got all the way back" from the pandemic, said Josh Lehner, a U.S. economist at SGH Macro Advisors.\par \par \uc2 While auto and chip makers have cut tens of thousands of workers over the past year, stable layoff rates across the sector suggest that the jobs pullback is gradual.\par \par \uc2 Lehner and other economists also said there are signs output has stabilized, if not inched higher, though gains in efficiency could limit the number of new jobs. A White House spokesman noted that manufacturing productivity ticked upward in recent quarters and that workers' wage hikes outpaced inflation over the past year.\par \par \uc2 In the long term, tariffs could achieve their desired effect of making some manufacturers more competitive with overseas producers. Economists believe lower interest rates and deregulation could also provide support. But in the shorter run, tariffs have boosted many companies' costs on materials sourced abroad, pushing firms that buy foreign parts to raise prices or scramble for supplies.\par \par \uc2 The White House's stop-and-start policymaking -- Trump threatened new tariffs on Europe, Canada and South Korea in recent weeks -- has also led to what many executives view as a lost year for investment. The possibility that the Supreme Court could nullify some import taxes has added to the uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, China and others have continued pumping out exports despite tariffs, pushing down prices in global markets where U.S. manufacturers are struggling to compete.\par \par \uc2 "There's very little in our product portfolio that has benefited from tariffs," said \uc2 H.O. Woltz III\uc2 , chief executive of North Carolina-based \uc2 Insteel Industries\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With tariffs on foreign steel doubled to 50% this year, Insteel has increasingly struggled to get from U.S. suppliers the metal it shapes into wire that reinforces concrete infrastructure, such as the Gordie Howe bridge, a major trade crossing that will soon connect Detroit to Canada. Instead, Insteel has at times turned to tariffed imports from Algeria, India and elsewhere when there weren't enough U.S. supplies to go around.\par \par \uc2 "Our growth today could be compromised by the dearth of [domestic] raw material available to us," Woltz said.\par \par \uc2 In the trucking industry, a yearslong postpandemic slide has dinged firms such as metal-component maker \uc2 NN\uc2 . The Charlotte, N.C.-based company, which runs 23 plants in six countries, trimmed its U.S. workforce in recent years to compete with low-cost factories abroad, as well as in response to slowing growth in electric-vehicle demand.\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Harold Bevis\uc2  believes tariffs will ultimately benefit \uc2 NN\uc2  by curbing Chinese competition for precision parts that appear in steering systems, audiovisual controls and more. But meanwhile, import taxes have helped push up costs for steel and aluminum, adding to pressures from soaring market prices for the gold and silver \uc2 NN\uc2  uses in some products.\par \par \uc2 That has squeezed how much cash the firm has to invest in new, potentially lucrative sectors such as data centers and electrical equipment.\par \par \uc2 "So you take a hit," Bevis said. \uc2 NN\uc2  is trying to recoup costs by raising prices in subsequent orders.\par \par \uc2 Bevis said business has accelerated while Ford and GM, which recently took multibillion-dollar write-downs on their EV businesses, have pushed for domestic parts. As \uc2 NN\uc2  evaluates where to expand manufacturing for the auto business, Bevis warned places such as Michigan and Massachusetts are still a hard sell compared with Mexico, where many products remain tariff-free through a trade deal.\par \par \uc2 Investing to supply the auto market in China -- home to three NN factories -- is also a safer bet than building out its footprint stateside.\par \par \uc2 Pointing to that country's parallel push to consolidate auto supply chains within its borders, Bevis said, "They are doing it at a way faster pace than the U.S."\par \par \uc2 Trump, a Republican, has taken other measures to try to jump-start the manufacturing sector. He muscled trading partners such as Japan and South Korea into deals that include pledges to invest hundreds of billions of dollars in the U.S. Firms such as Apple, TSMC and AstraZeneca have announced massive projects that could create thousands of manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials said the long-term vision is to make industries self-sufficient. The timeline for investments is often years, muddling the near-term outlook for manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know when all of this money is going to kick in," Trump said in a December interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that new investments will likely focus on the robotic tools and artificial-intelligence components that have captivated Wall Street, meaning a surge in new, permanent factory jobs is less likely.\par \par \uc2 Some investors believe interest-rate cuts and stimulative fiscal policy should help the economy grow faster this year.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest factor overall in how manufacturing is doing is how our economy is doing. There's no getting away from that," said Scott Paul, president of the Alliance for American Manufacturing, which supports tariffs on steel and many Chinese products. "It's way too early to tell what the new normal is because we've just exited the roller-coaster ride."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich422\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6330
