\par\par \b \uc2 Labor Shortage Hits Broadband Boom --- Record demand for fiber-optic cables means workers can pull in soaring pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patience Haggin \par \uc2 872 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 EAGAR, Ariz. -- Sammy Collins twists and pulls on a directional drill's controls. He uses the 15,000-pound vehicle to bore a path through the soil for future fiber-optic lines. About 40 other members of his crew across this rural corner of Apache County dig ditches and hang fiber cables on poles.\par \par \uc2 Collins, who previously worked at an addiction treatment facility, joined this crew six months ago, notching two promotions and boosting his pay by more than 22%. Experienced drillers can make anywhere from the high $20s to mid-$40s an hour.\par \par \uc2 Fiber cables were laid at a record number of homes last year, fueled by billions in federal grants for broadband internet. Technology companies' appetite for data centers is boosting demand further.\par \par \uc2 Yet the rush to build is hitting a human bottleneck, particularly among workers who can piece together the fiber networks at the heart of the technology. Crews can't hire fast enough and workers often are poached by competitors.\par \par \uc2 "We just don't have the people to do the work," said Kyle Braude, who manages fiber build projects for JKL Associates, a subsidiary of utility-construction contractor Push. The biggest holdup in the fiber boom, he said, is human capital for roles including drillers, foremen, splicers and aerial linemen.\par \par \uc2 The demand is expected to create 58,000 new jobs between 2025 and 2032, according to a 2024 report by the \uc2 Fiber Broadband Association\uc2  and the Power & Communication Contractors Association. With 120,000 workers expected to leave the field in the same period -- mainly through retirement -- the report forecasts a combined shortage of 178,000 workers. As a result, contractors and analysts predict our AI future might take longer to achieve and a goal of nationwide high-speed internet could remain out of reach.\par \par \uc2 For those with fiber-laying skills, the work can pay good money, no college needed. Telecommunications line installers and repairers made annual median wages of $70,500 for the year ended May 2024, the latest figure available, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . That figure doesn't include overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 Push raised hourly wages for fiber crews by 5% to 8% in each of the past several years, and expects pay to rise even faster, said Chase Lapcinski, Push president.\par \par \uc2 Entry-level fiber workers at Jonesburg, Mo.-based Sellenriek Construction made about $60,000 including overtime last year. That can jump by 25% to 30% in the first three years, said Chief Executive Steve Sellenriek, and site superintendents can make six figures. Sellenriek said he is seeing an unprecedented rush of clients trying to lock in contracts early, before wages rise even further.\par \par \uc2 Many workers join crews without prior fiber experience, the way Collins did. Others take training courses before getting hired. Learning Alliance in Tampa, Fla., offers a six-week to eight-week boot camp, funded by military benefits and corporate donations, that trains students to install fiber. The program's 2025 graduates made about $26 an hour in starting pay, plus overtime, said Cesar Ruiz, Learning Alliance's president and CEO. He has seen that rate increase by about 13% in two years.\par \par \uc2 Fiber crews often work more than 10 hours a day outdoors in nearly all weather. They may relocate to a project area for months at a time. And winter can bring less work in parts of the country, because of challenges drilling through snow and frozen ground.\par \par \uc2 Mitch Grant, a fiber splicer with K&S Communications, has been spending time lately in Springerville, Ariz. Most locals there don't have high-speed fiber internet, and he says they are eager to get connected -- so eager, they stop him at work and ask for updates. (The service, provided by Commnet's Ethos Broadband, is expected to be in place within two to four months.)\par \par \uc2 Grant's work is delicate: He cleaves each strand of a fiber-optic cable for a clean edge, then uses a machine to fuse the hair-thin filaments, which carry digital data at the speed of light. A slight misalignment, or even a speck of oil from the technician's hands, and the strands won't join.\par \par \uc2 "When you first start doing it, it's like, 'How can you possibly do it?' But then you get muscle memory," Grant said. Four years ago, he left cell tower work to manage fiber crews, then saw how much this particular specialty makes -- sometimes upward of $30 per splice for projects like this -- and moved into that role.\par \par \uc2 Skilled trades across the board are suffering a workforce pinch, and fiber doesn't have the training pipeline that trades like auto repair and electrical power work enjoy.\par \par \uc2 Lapcinski of Push is working to change that. He hosts hands-on skill days for high-school students in his home state of Wisconsin, so teens can get exposure. He hired farmers, former inmates and white-collar workers tired of office life.\par \par \uc2 "AI isn't going to replace this," Lapcinski said. "It will accelerate and support some of it, but it's not going to go out and dig the hole."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260202em220000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
