\par\par \b \uc2 Uber Eats\uc2  Ordered To Pay $3.5 Million\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Hamilton \par \uc2 268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Uber Technologies\uc2 ' food-delivery business agreed to pay $3.5 million in worker pay restitution and civil penalties as part of an order by the New York City government.\par \par \uc2 New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani and the Department of Consumer and Worker Protection ordered \uc2 Uber Eats\uc2  to pay $3.15 million in restitution to 48,000 workers across the city, and $350,000 in civil penalties and fees.\par \par \uc2 An investigation by DCWP alleged that \uc2 Uber Eats\uc2  failed to pay workers the minimum pay rate between December 2023 and September 2024 for time spent on canceled trips, the mayor's office said Friday.\par \par \uc2 A DCWP report said \uc2 Uber\uc2  and other food-delivery companies engineered their interfaces to lower workers' tip earnings by $550 million.\par \par \uc2 "We're glad to have this resolved," \uc2 Uber\uc2  communications director Josh Gold said. "After the city notified us of the issue in August of 2024, we immediately corrected it, agreed to pay more than the amount owed, and appreciate the new administration moving quickly to bring this to a fair conclusion."\par \par \uc2 Food-delivery platforms Fantuan and \uc2 HungryPanda\uc2  are also being ordered to make payments alongside \uc2 Uber Eats\uc2  for a total amount of $5.2 million.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  also agreed to reinstate workers who were wrongfully deactivated, which could be as many as 10,000 people, the mayor's office said. \uc2 Uber\uc2  has reactivated 1,053 workers, Gold said. Gold said just over 94,000 couriers were impacted with an average underpayment of $19.48.\par \par \uc2 DCWP Commissioner Sam Levine recently sent compliance warnings to more than 60 delivery 