\par\par \b \uc2 Business Travelers Enter New Era Of $100 Rooms and Meatloaf --- Scrimping is in high gear as companies cut spending; Cracker Barrel's road rules\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 898 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The message from Cracker Barrel management went down for some like a stale biscuit.\par \par \uc2 Employees should postpone work travel until later this year, when the Southern-themed restaurant chain hopes to start recovering from a steep sales slide. But if workers must hit the road, management said, they should fuel up on the chain's own meatloaf and country fried steak.\par \par \uc2 "Employees are expected to dine at a Cracker Barrel store for all or the majority of meals while traveling, whenever practical based on location and schedule," the company said in an internal message.\par \par \uc2 "Travelscrimping" is in high gear across corporate America. Companies looking for cuts are turning their focus on the expenses employees incur on the road. The changes are stripping work trips of whatever shred of glamour they had after years of belt-tightening.\par \par \uc2 Rather than enjoying one of the few remaining perks of white-collar work, employees are scouring for $100 hotel rooms, preparing grocery-store-bought meals and trekking to laundromats to wash their dress shirts rather than expensing the hotel's white-glove service.\par \par \uc2 On top of the dining guidelines, Cracker Barrel is cutting off its traveling workers from booze, with any alcohol needing to be covered personally. "Exceptions for special occasions must be pre-approved by an E-Team member," the company wrote.\par \par \uc2 Inside Cracker Barrel, where the company held layoffs recently, some workers shrugged off the change as minor compared with their broader fears, saying they didn't even think they could drink when traveling. (Cracker Barrel started adding alcohol to its menus in 2021.)\par \par \uc2 Many companies still want their road warriors to travel, but more employees expect to pick up the tab for costs as budgets shrink, according to a recent survey by SAP Concur.\par \par \uc2 Justin Salerno used to go with his gut on spending decisions while traveling for an aerospace startup. After his firm was bought recently, the Milwaukee-area engineer now must line up his expenses with the federal rate used to reimburse employees for lodging and meals.\par \par \uc2 It's a test of accounting and bargain-hunting skills. Hotel rooms in an area of Utah he frequents should be no more than $110. When the city has special events, those rooms can cost several hundred dollars a night. The 45-year-old typically decides comfort and convenience are worth the cost of having to explain to his managers why he went over the limit.\par \par \uc2 "I'm a bit loosey goosey about it," he said.\par \par \uc2 Companies have plenty of good reasons to keep a lid on travel costs. Times are tight, and expense-account abuse can be a drain.\par \par \uc2 As a finance chief for construction companies, Jeff Oscarson has seen employees try to push through some logic-defying travel expenses, including a $500 bottle of wine, pairs of jeans and a chair.\par \par \uc2 "Why would you do something to poke the bear by expensing a chair?" Oscarson said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has long asked corporate workers to travel frugally, suggesting the cheap seats when possible. The policy has sparked internal debate about what is cheap enough, and if being really tall warrants business class for overseas trips.\par \par \uc2 David Markley, a 58-year-old executive coach who spent seven years at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , repeatedly flew coach from Seattle to Europe, Japan and Bengaluru. He learned to never sit near the bulkhead where bassinets frequently were placed.\par \par \uc2 "When one baby wakes up, they all tend to wake up," he said.\par \par \uc2 Some corporate travelers say they are getting used to foraging for their meals at grocery stores and assembling them back at their hotels.\par \par \uc2 Others skimp on breakfast and lunch and try to save their allowance of dollars and calories for dinner.\par \par \uc2 Leroy Craighead, 49, a business consultant from Chicago, has adopted a system for surviving on a tight client budget. He limits himself to a coffee from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in the morning and a spartan lunch to free up funds for a satisfying dinner. He's also mastered using his frequent-flier status with various hotels to score dinners at their members-only lounges.\par \par \uc2 "There's tricks," Craighead said.\par \par \uc2 Corporate road warriors say European companies can be even more parsimonious. Michael Paci, a senior manager at an international consulting firm, tries to be reasonable, but doesn't think much of charging a $50 steak meal with a beer. His European colleagues have to save receipts even for a drip coffee.\par \par \uc2 "They are going to get you two euros at a time. There is a degree of absurdity," he said.\par \par \uc2 Joe Fenti, a 29-year-old Boston resident, quit his corporate consulting job last year after feeling like his life was being whittled away by busywork with no concrete end. He's channeling the experience into a full-time comedy career; part of his routine is acting out interactions between managers and workers.\par \par \uc2 "We changed the expense policy. We notified everyone via smoke signals and bird calls," Fenti's fictional manager said in one TikTok video.\par \par \uc2 "My report is just Ubers and diners from a client trip, this really shouldn't be hard," the employee responded.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, having run out of ideas to reconcile his expenses, the employee resorts to using a random code. "This is perfect," the manager said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260131em1v0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
