\par\par \b \uc2 Layoffs Reverse Pandemic Job Boom\b0\par\par\uc2 By Konrad Putzier and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,247 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The new era of corporate cost-cutting is hitting U.S. workers with full force.\par \par \uc2 Big companies from \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  to UPS are slashing jobs, looking to shrink their head counts after years of breakneck growth. Companies generously expanded their workforces during the pandemic years of 2020 and 2021 and doled out hefty raises, worried that moving too slowly might leave them with a shortage of skilled workers.\par \par \uc2 Now, some companies said their hiring sprees went too far. Their biggest concerns today are bloat and runaway expenses. "A lot of these companies found that they are too big," said Guy Berger, a senior fellow at the think tank Burning Glass Institute.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said Wednesday that it would lay off an additional 16,000 corporate employees after laying off 14,000 workers in the fall. The combined cuts amount to about 10% of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's corporate workforce. On Tuesday, UPS said it expected to slash 30,000 jobs this year, on top of 48,000 job cuts last year, saying the company needed to "right-size." Also on Tuesday, social-media company \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  said it planned to shrink its workforce by up to 15%.\par \par \uc2 For months, corporate executives and labor economists have theorized about whether -- and when -- advances in artificial intelligence will lead to widespread layoffs. But that shoe has yet to meaningfully drop.\par \par \uc2 For the moment at least, the layoff story is centered on bloat. The sectors that recorded some of the top hiring surges in 2020 and 2021 -- tech and logistics -- are now seeing the most layoffs. U.S.-based employers announced 1.2 million job cuts in 2025, the highest annual figure since 2020, according to outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 . The tech sector led all private-sector industries with 154,445 job cuts, followed by warehousing with 95,317.\par \par \uc2 "This is still about the overhiring or hiring boom that happened in that immediate postpandemic era," said Laura Ullrich, director of economic research at jobs-platform Indeed.\par \par \uc2 CEOs insist behind the scenes that their companies are still too large and too bloated. In announcing staff cuts, many executives have said the layoffs would speed up operations and give people more autonomy. \uc2 Beth Galetti\uc2 , a senior vice president of people experience and technology at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , wrote in a note to staff Wednesday that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's cuts were aimed at trimming management layers and removing bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 "Some of you might ask if this is the beginning of a new rhythm -- where we announce broad reductions every few months," Galetti wrote. "That's not our plan."\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  said the company likely would be hiring fewer people in its corporate-support centers, with more job growth showing up in the chain's cafes. Niccol said in an interview Wednesday that the company needed to examine where it could get more efficient through technology when it came to its staffing. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has held two rounds of layoffs since Niccol joined as CEO in September 2024.\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  CEO \uc2 Ted Decker\uc2  told employees the retailer would cut 800 roles and require that corporate employees work five days a week in its offices beginning in April, part of an attempt to simplify the company's structure and operate faster.\par \par \uc2 By many measures, the U.S. economy still boasts a relatively healthy job market. The layoffs are heavily concentrated among a small number of big companies, and overall job losses are low by historical standards. The unemployment rate, while up from 2024, is well below prepandemic levels. As prominent employers such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  now cut roles, many companies in other industries continue to hold on to their staffers.\par \par \uc2 Hiring has nevertheless slowed to a crawl. People who lose their job today often have a hard time finding a new one, and they stay out of work for longer. In December, the average length of unemployment was 24.4 weeks, according to Labor Department data. In December 2022, the figure was 19.4 weeks.\par \par \uc2 High interest rates and tariff uncertainty are weighing on the jobs picture, leading more companies to hit the pause button on hiring, said Lisa Simon, chief economist at workforce data company Revelio Labs.\par \par \uc2 Firms also are investing billions of dollars in AI, shifting expenses to technology and leaving less for labor.\par \par \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  said on Tuesday it would cut up to 15% of its workforce, or about 700 jobs, in an effort to shift its resources to AI-focused roles and products. The company said in a filing that it is "prioritizing AI-powered products and capabilities," and would also be speeding up a transformation of its sales approach.\par \par \uc2 The chemicals giant Dow also said Thursday that it would cut 4,500 positions as part of a cost-savings program that will rely on AI to help deliver greater productivity.\par \par \uc2 Some economists and CEOs said that the U.S. economy is positioned only at the beginning of a wave of corporate job cuts, as rising AI-adoption makes more workers expendable. Economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimate that AI was responsible for 5,000 to 10,000 monthly net job losses in the industries most exposed to it in 2025. They expect the figure to rise to 20,000 a month in 2026. Over time, AI could displace 6% to 7% of all current jobs, the bank's economists wrote in a recent note, although AI likely will create new jobs by boosting economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Verizon Communications CEO Dan Schulman, on a panel at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, said last week that the job market was shifting so rapidly that more turmoil was all but inevitable. The company in November said it planned to cut more than 13,000 jobs, its largest reduction ever.\par \par \uc2 "If you say to your employees there's not going to be any job disruption, I think you lose all credibility," Schulman said. "All of them get it that there's going to be. It's really early on -- and a year from now, it's going to be radically different."\par \par \uc2 He said: "Machines can do most anything we do better than we can do it."\par \par \uc2 Still, at the moment, companies are pointing fingers at years of robust hiring. Logistics firms expanded heavily during the pandemic as consumers spent more money ordering anything from stationary bikes to restaurant meals online. But recently, tariff uncertainty and trade tensions have weighed on the sector, economists said, while automation means fewer workers are needed.\par \par \uc2 Nike said this week it would cut 775 people, primarily at distribution centers across Tennessee and Mississippi, as the sneaker company rolls out more advanced automation and looks to streamline its operations. The reductions represent about 1% of Nike's overall workforce and are part of a broader turnaround effort to help it return to long-term, profitable growth, the company said.\par \par \uc2 "I do think we're going to find more streamlining of large organizations for the foreseeable future, and that will create different jobs," said Andy Decker, CEO of the recruiting firm Goodwin Recruiting, which works with employers across different industries.\par \par \uc2 When Decker and his colleagues talk with people looking for work, they have emphasized that they might need to pursue different roles in the future.\par \par \uc2 "In this current market, people need to realize what they've done might not be what they're going to be doing forward," he said. "They've got to constantly be evolving."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich297\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4455
