\par\par \b \uc2 Dow\uc2  to Cut Jobs In AI Overhaul\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rob Curran \par \uc2 199 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dow\uc2  is cutting 4,500 employees as part of a cost-saving program that will lean on artificial intelligence to increase productivity and bolster shareholder returns.\par \par \uc2 The cuts, which will result in $1.1 billion to $1.5 billion in one-time charges, came as the chemicals company said its quarterly loss widened because of lower revenue and higher costs.\par \par \uc2 Dow\uc2  said it was embarking on a program dubbed Transform to Outperform, which would employ AI and automation to reduce expenditure and catalyze growth and productivity, yielding an additional $2 billion in operating earnings before interest, depreciation and amortization.\par \par \uc2 Chairman and CEO \uc2 Jim Fitterling\uc2  said the program "represents a comprehensive and radical simplification of our operating model."\par \par \uc2 Separately on Thursday, \uc2 Dow\uc2  posted a quarterly loss of $1.48 billion, or $2.15 a share, wider than $35 million, or 8 cents a share, a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Dow\uc2  logged an adjusted loss, excluding certain one-off items, of 34 cents a share, compared with the mean analyst estimate of a 50 cents a share loss.\par \par \uc2 Sales fell 9.1% to $9.46 billion, meeting the average analyst forecast, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich423\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6345
