\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 Construction Firms Turn to AI to Help Manage Projects\b0\par\par\uc2 By Belle Lin \par \uc2 737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The construction industry is looking to AI and AI agents to make project managers' lives easier, and eventually address an exodus of retiring talent.\par \par \uc2 Construction-software firms are selling AI-powered tools that can assist site managers and foremen with work like analyzing sites for potential safety violations -- tasks AI models can accomplish by searching through and synthesizing large amounts of data. And they are laying groundwork for AI that can one day reproduce the knowledge of an experienced leader or skilled worker.\par \par \uc2 Such technology comes at a critical time. The data-center boom is resulting in an uptick in demand for skilled builders. Meanwhile, the industry is short about 349,000 workers, according to January 2026 data from the Associated Builders and Contractors trade group. About 41% of the current construction workforce is projected to retire by 2031, the National Center for Construction Research and Education found.\par \par \uc2 Procore Technologies\uc2 , which sells construction-management software, unveiled a slew of AI agents last year that aim to help with daily construction site to-do's, including a log agent, a foreman's personal assistant and an action item generator for back-office tasks.\par \par \uc2 The Carpinteria, Calif.-based company, founded in 2002, reported $339 million in revenue in its most recent quarter. \uc2 Procore\uc2 's AI tools rely on a variety of models, including those from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company's Assist tool taps large-language models to summarize jobsite information and other documents, so project managers can find answers hidden in their data, such as the fire-rating requirements for a specific room or wall. Other \uc2 Procore\uc2  tools use AI to extract data from project documents to automatically create a list of required submittals.\par \par \uc2 Procore\uc2  is one of several software providers for the construction sector, and among the largest that specializes in project management. Others include \uc2 Trimble\uc2  and \uc2 Autodesk\uc2 , as well as smaller startups using AI to tackle construction scheduling and design-based tasks.\par \par \uc2 AI also has other uses in construction: The startups AI Clearing and OpenSpace use computer vision to track the progress of construction sites based on aerial and other real-world images. Skillit, a New York City-based startup, is using AI and AI agents to match construction workers with open positions.\par \par \uc2 One reason AI tools for project management help save time is because they support natural language processing, enabling managers and other site workers to speak directly to the apps on their devices.\par \par \uc2 Dictating into \uc2 Procore\uc2 's AI tool lets Minneapolis-based Mortenson's superintendents record their daily logs while they drive around a project site, said Gene Hodge, the construction firm's vice president of innovation. "If you can input data that way, and not have to do it in front of a computer, having that user interface improvement is really great," he said.\par \par \uc2 Kaufman Lynn, a Florida-based construction firm, built an agent using \uc2 Procore\uc2 's technology that pulls data from different tools and automatically creates a formatted monthly progress report. "Something that took us six to eight hours is done within minutes," said Tim Bonczek, an executive vice president at the firm.\par \par \uc2 Sixty percent of construction firms are already using some form of AI, though most of those are large companies, according to research from \uc2 International Data Corp.\uc2  Many smaller construction providers are still digitizing and were moving to the cloud just a few years ago, said Jeff Hojlo, an analyst at \uc2 IDC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, AI could serve as a way to save and transfer the knowledge of skilled workers -- especially those who retire or exit the sector. "We have a number of people retiring in our sports division that have built a lot of stadiums, and we're talking about, 'How do we capture all of the knowledge that they've built?'" Mortenson's Hodge said.\par \par \uc2 When construction firms start to use AI, it also forces them to create the data the technology can use later, said Aviad Almagor, \uc2 Trimble\uc2 's vice president of technology innovation. "Historically, this data sometimes just resides in the heads of those experienced employees," he said,\par \par \uc2 Construction firm \uc2 Skanska\uc2  is already trying to use AI to share the knowledge of a few skilled experts with the entire company. But before AI agents can help with knowledge transfer, people need to trust the technology.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Belle Lin writes WSJ Leadership Institute's CIO Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260128em1s0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
