\par\par \b \uc2 Nike\uc2  Lays Off 775 Workers in Automation Bid\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kelly Cloonan \par \uc2 192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  is laying off about 775 workers as it ramps up its use of advanced technology and automation.\par \par \uc2 The cuts will primarily affect workers at its distribution centers across Tennessee and Mississippi and amount to about 1% of its overall workforce. \uc2 Nike\uc2  employed about 77,800 workers worldwide as of its latest annual filing.\par \par \uc2 The move is part of \uc2 Nike\uc2 's efforts to strengthen and streamline operations, the company said.\par \par \uc2 "We are sharpening our supply-chain footprint, accelerating the use of advanced technology and automation, and investing in the skills our teams need for the future," \uc2 Nike\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The cuts also aim to help \uc2 Nike\uc2  return to long-term profitable growth and boost margins over time, the company said.\par \par \uc2 "These actions are designed to reduce complexity, improve flexibility, and build a more responsive, resilient, responsible, and efficient operation," \uc2 Nike\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  has sought to gain ground on its turnaround despite tariffs and weakness in China damping its efforts. The company recently shook up its leadership, working to be more nimble like competitors that have gained ground in the sneaker and athleticwear market.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260127em1r00011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
