\par\par \b \uc2 ICE Standoff Places CEOs in a Tough Spot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter and Sarah Nassauer \par \uc2 1,123 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CEOs have tried their hardest to stay on the political sidelines since President Trump started his second term -- and mostly succeeded. Now the pushback against the immigration crackdown in Minneapolis is pulling more of them in.\par \par \uc2 Some of the country's biggest companies, including 3M, \uc2 Medtronic\uc2 , \uc2 Ecolab\uc2 , \uc2 U.S. Bancorp\uc2  and \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2 , all have headquarters and thousands of employees in the Twin Cities. Though they have kept largely quiet in public, many of their leaders are trying to navigate the charged issue with employees and community members behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 Target -- Minneapolis's best-known corporate brand -- is a case in point. The hometown retailer hasn't issued any public statements since the detention of two local store workers, both U.S. citizens, earlier this month. But its executives agreed to meet with a group of local clergy who protested in the lobby of the company's headquarters this month.\par \par \uc2 The company also has circulated updated guidelines for how staff in stores and warehouses should respond to "unannounced immigration-related contacts," according to documents viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Agents are permitted to speak to people in the store in public areas, and workers can't interfere with the agents or help others hide, the document says. In addition, only top managers should interact with immigration agents, and they should immediately alert corporate officials. Agents need a judicial warrant to enter private areas of stores, such as backrooms. Remain calm, the guidelines add.\par \par \uc2 Hilton Worldwide Holdings\uc2  appeared earlier this month to have nipped a similar public-relations crisis in the bud. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  had accused the hotel chain of a "coordinated campaign" to refuse service to \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  officers after a Hampton Inn in its network turned away some agents. \uc2 Hilton\uc2  responded swiftly, removing the independently owned hotel from its system and reiterating that its properties were open to everyone.\par \par \uc2 But that wasn't the end of it. Within days, an immigration officer fatally shot Renee Nicole Good, and the Trump administration sent more ICE agents to the Twin Cities, ratcheting up tensions across the metro area. Last weekend, \uc2 Hilton\uc2  backed another local hotel owner's decision to temporarily shut a hotel where immigration officers had allegedly booked rooms. The DoubleTree property had received bomb threats, and its owners didn't feel comfortable operating a hotel under those circumstances, Hilton CEO \uc2 Chris Nassetta\uc2  said in his first public comments on the matter.\par \par \uc2 "A safety and security issue is a different issue -- it's closed to all," Nassetta said. He said that he had been deeply involved in the company's decision-making and was receiving twice-daily reports on social-media attention on the matter.\par \par \uc2 Though he said it isn't \uc2 Hilton\uc2 's place to wade into politically divisive issues, that is not so easy in this case. "It's hard to argue it's not a little bit more complicated than normal."\par \par \uc2 Companies' cautious efforts to handle the political challenge marks a new chapter for some in dealing with the Trump administration. Even before Trump's second term, many had retreated from reflexively issuing statements on hot-button issues like immigration and LGBT rights because of blowback worries. The sustained backlash against Bud Light after its social-media promotion with a transgender advocate a few years ago especially spooked companies.\par \par \uc2 Now some face the kind of employee pressure to speak out that was common during Trump's first term. An online letter that circulated this past week -- signed by hundreds of employees of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's \uc2 Google\uc2  and other technology companies -- asks tech CEOs to use their influence with the White House to demand ICE officials leave cities. It points out that tech CEOs successfully used their leverage last year to prevent Trump from sending the National Guard to San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment. A spokeswoman for \uc2 Google\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Americans themselves are all over the place on how businesses should respond. In a survey conducted by \uc2 Morning Consult\uc2  this past week, about a third of respondents said companies should comply with legal requirements but not assist immigration agents beyond that. Just over 20% said companies should actively resist ICE actions. The same percentage said they were unsure what companies should do.\par \par \uc2 "Business doesn't have to weigh in on politics," said \uc2 Richard Edelman\uc2 , head of public-relations firm \uc2 Edelman\uc2 . "However, when there is an important incident in a city, you need to be involved with your people so that they know that you're a community member just as they are: that you're empathetic, that you are a leader not just for the numbers, but for the heart."\par \par \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2 , based in the Minneapolis suburbs, hasn't made a public statement on immigration actions in the city, but it is closely tracking the movement of immigration officials and protesters to keep its workers safe, a person familiar with the situation said. The company is sending frequent updates about areas that might be affected and allowing staff to work remotely if they feel unsafe, the person said. A UnitedHealth spokesman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 In the Minneapolis area, some activist groups called for a general strike on Friday, urging people not to work, shop or attend school in protest of ICE's actions. Some small businesses have added signs to doors that immigration officials aren't welcome, or have locked their doors so that they can control who comes in.\par \par \uc2 A door sign that says "ICE not welcome" generally doesn't override laws on what areas are considered open to the public, said Michael Neifach, a principal at law firm JacksonLewis and former principal legal adviser for ICE from 2007 to 2009, during the Bush administration.\par \par \uc2 For companies wanting to be responsive to employees' concerns, knowing what to say to reflect the moment can be its own challenge. Target has encouraged leaders to check in with their staff and emphasize the flexibility workers have to work from home if they prefer, people familiar with the situation said.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 8, the day after Good was killed, Target's head of human resources, Melissa Kremer, sent an email to all Minneapolis staff.\par \par \uc2 "I wanted to take a moment to reach out to you in light of the events in our hometown," the email started. The company was watching the situation carefully as "we receive new information by the moment," she said. Safety and following all laws were a priority, she said.\par \par \uc2 "We recognize that situations like that can bring a wide range of emotions and reactions, and that everyone may experience them differently," she said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260124em1o0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
