\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Slowing Job Market Becomes Personal for Economists\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists will tell you that it is a tough time for people entering the job market. They can relate: It is the worst market young economists have faced in memory.\par \par \uc2 The economics job market is getting buffeted by a confluence of forces. Worries about federal funding have led many major universities to reduce, or even freeze, hiring. Jobs within the federal government have dried up. The private sector, where demand for economists has been intense in recent years, has pulled back.\par \par \uc2 "It's like a perfect storm," said \uc2 Syracuse University\uc2  economist John Cawley, who heads the \uc2 American Economic Association\uc2 's job market committee.\par \par \uc2 The tough market for doctoral students finishing up their studies is hardly unique to economics. What's different about economists is that they are intensely interested in measuring and understanding how labor markets work -- and they have brought that to bear on their own profession. As a result, armed with data from the AEA and all the knowledge they gained in graduate school, doctoral students in economics have a much more precise grasp of what type of environment they are facing than their counterparts in political science, philosophy or biophysics.\par \par \uc2 The AEA functions as the main clearinghouse for economics jobs through its Job Openings for Economists network, or JOE. Institutions both in the U.S. and abroad post openings there.\par \par \uc2 Openings are way down. By the AEA's count, as of December there were 1,773 job openings for Ph.D. economists. That wasn't just below the levels of a year earlier, when demand for economists was already weak; it was also down by about 20% from the same point in 2020 -- the year the pandemic buckled the market.\par \par \uc2 That data is included in a series of charts on the AEA's website for graduate students to stare at. Or not stare at, as the case may be.\par \par \uc2 "Some people are like, 'I am not going to look at the graphs, the graphs don't exist for me until May,'" said Danielle Graves Williamson, a \uc2 Boston University\uc2  doctoral student who is on the market.\par \par \uc2 The weakness is broad-based. Job openings for full-time academic positions are down by 33% from a year earlier, according to the AEA. There are only 24 federal government job listings, down from 84 a year earlier. The number of job openings in business and finance has been soft for a third year running.\par \par \uc2 Typically, the job market has no problem absorbing the roughly 1,300 Ph.D.s that U.S. economics programs mint each year.\par \par \uc2 The value of an economics Ph.D. has risen over the past quarter-century. The analytical skills the field teaches have made it a popular major for undergraduates, business schools have grown, and other academic areas, such as schools of health policy, have taken on economists.\par \par \uc2 The pay can be very good. The \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 's economics department -- one of the top programs in the world -- lists starting pay for a junior, tenure-track professor at $225,000 to $254,000.\par \par \uc2 But the reality for economists on the job market now is much different than what they might have imagined when they applied to graduate school five or six years ago.\par \par \uc2 "I thought Covid was bad, but this is much worse than Covid," said Donna Ginther, a \uc2 University of Kansas\uc2  economist who earned her doctorate in 1995. "It is the worst job market I've ever seen."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich443\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6645
