\par\par \b \uc2 Autodesk\uc2  Restructuring Set To Eliminate 1,000 Positions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robb M. Stewart \par \uc2 204 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Autodesk\uc2  plans to cut about 1,000 jobs, roughly 7% of its workforce, as part of a global restructuring plan as it seeks to strengthen sales and marketing.\par \par \uc2 The move is the final phase of efforts by the maker of AutoCAD and other digital design software to streamline customer engagement and bolster its sales channels. The plan also reallocates resources in certain other functions to accelerate strategic priorities, \uc2 Autodesk\uc2  said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The company said a significant portion of the job cuts will be within customer-facing sales functions.\par \par \uc2 Autodesk\uc2  forecast the move will result in pretax restructuring charges of about $135 million to $160 million, largely tied to employee termination benefits. Most of the pretax restructuring charges will result in cash expenditures during the fiscal year through Jan. 31, 2027, it said. The company said it expected to record before-tax restructuring charges of roughly $90 million to $110 million in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2026, and the remainder the following year.\par \par \uc2 Autodesk\uc2  said it expected to complete the plan by the end of fiscal 2027, subject to local law and consultation requirements.\par \par \uc2 Shares gained 4.8% to $269.77 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260123em1n0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
