\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Ford Worker Is Suspended for Heckling Trump During Factory Visit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 451 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  suspended a worker who heckled President Trump during a tour of a Michigan truck factory on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 T.J. Sabula shouted out "pedophile protector" as Trump walked past. Trump visited the plant to tout efforts to bolster U.S. auto manufacturing. The president responded by pointing and raising his middle finger, according to a video posted online of the interaction.\par \par \uc2 The suspension is the latest reminder of the limitations of speech in the workplace, where employers -- including tech giants such as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  as well as retailer \uc2 Office Depot\uc2  -- have taken action against employees who took political stands on the job.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, Hilton cut ties with one property because staff declined to book rooms for federal immigration agents.\par \par \uc2 Sabula, a 40-year-old line worker, couldn't be reached for comment. After the event, Sabula told the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  that he was about 60 feet from Trump when he yelled out. "Definitely no regrets whatsoever," he said.\par \par \uc2 The White House said Trump's response was appropriate. "A lunatic was wildly screaming expletives in a complete fit of rage, and the president gave an appropriate and unambiguous response," said \uc2 Steven Cheung\uc2 , White House communications director.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Sabula's \uc2 Facebook\uc2  profile photo was an image of Trump with a raised middle finger and the page included some previously posted anti-Trump political cartoons. Sabula didn't vote in the 2024 election, voting records show, though he is registered in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 , a Democrat who represents a district near Detroit, said she has spoken with Sabula. "He's rattled. He's fearful for his family," the congresswoman said. "I hope the company doesn't fire him."\par \par \uc2 Dingell said it isn't uncommon for politicians to get heckled on factory floors. "Let's take a breath," she said.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, then-presidential candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a testy exchange at a Detroit plant that also became a viral video. After a worker at a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant asked him about his gun policy, Biden told the worker "don't be such a horse's ass."\par \par \uc2 Ford declined to discuss Sabula's status. A spokesman said the company held a successful event with Trump and is proud of how employees represented the company. "One of our core values is respect and we don't condone anyone saying anything inappropriate like that within our facilities," a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Sabula is a member of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 , which said in a statement: "He believes in freedom of speech, a principle we wholeheartedly embrace, and we stand with our membership in protecting their voice on the job."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260115em1f0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
