\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Older Americans Face Tax Hit Under Catch-Up 401(k) Change --- A new rule requires many workers 50 and older to put extra savings into a Roth account\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Tergesen \par \uc2 1,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Older Americans making catch-up contributions to their 401(k) plans could be hit with a higher tax bill this year.\par \par \uc2 Under a law that went into effect on Jan. 1, higher-income workers making catch-up contributions to their 401(k) accounts will be required to put the extra money into a Roth 401(k) account. That means they will lose the upfront tax breaks for these extra contributions that traditional 401(k) accounts offer -- a benefit that is valuable to many in their peak earning years.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to materially affect this group of people," said Matthew Petersen, executive director of the National Association of Government Defined Contribution Administrators. Yet he said few employees know it's happening.\par \par \uc2 Some 401(k) plans will automatically make the adjustment for affected workers. But others are requiring employees to consent to the switch, which means they risk losing out on catch-up contributions if they don't act.\par \par \uc2 Here's what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 What are catch-up contributions?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  lets older workers make catch-up contributions to their 401(k)s to enhance their nest eggs as they near retirement. This year, people 50 and older can contribute up to an extra $8,000, on top of the $24,500 limit for people of all ages.\par \par \uc2 Workers ages 60 to 63 can contribute an extra $11,250.\par \par \uc2 Who is affected by the new Roth requirement?\par \par \uc2 The change affects older workers who earned more than $150,000 last year, including retirement contributions.\par \par \uc2 The wage limit is based on your prior year's income with your current employer. As a result, higher earners hired in 2026 can still make pretax catch-up contributions this year, but would likely be subject to the Roth mandate in 2027, said Ian Berger, an analyst with Ed Slott & Co., which provides advice on individual retirement accounts.\par \par \uc2 High-earners who are self-employed don't have W-2 wages from an employer and so are exempt from the Roth requirement, said Berger.\par \par \uc2 The new rule also applies to the $8,000 and $11,250 catch-up contributions to 403(b) and government 457(b) plans. It doesn't apply to IRAs.\par \par \uc2 How does the new rule change how catch-up contributions are taxed?\par \par \uc2 With traditional 401(k) accounts, savers subtract contributions from their income, reducing taxes. They then pay income taxes on withdrawals from their accounts in retirement, when many assume they will be in a lower tax bracket.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Roth\uc2  accounts, workers contribute after-tax income but build a pot of money they can withdraw tax-free in retirement. That will be valuable in years when tapping other accounts would push them into a higher tax bracket or force them to pay higher Medicare premiums.\par \par \uc2 Most 401(k) plans now include both traditional and Roth accounts.\par \par \uc2 I am affected by the Roth mandate. What do I need to do?\par \par \uc2 That depends on your plan, said Rob Austin, head of thought leadership at \uc2 Alight Solutions\uc2 , a 401(k) administrator that serves over 200 large plans.\par \par \uc2 Some retirement plans are automatically switching high earners to Roth catch-up contributions. Others are requiring higher earners to consent to the switch. Those who fail to do so could have their catch-up contributions suspended, said Austin.\par \par \uc2 He recommended that workers check with their plan administrator to find out whether they need to provide consent.\par \par \uc2 About two-thirds of the 401(k)-type plans that use \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  as an administrator are requiring higher earners to take action if they want their catch-ups to continue, by consenting to the Roth. By contrast, most of the plans that use \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2  are automating the Roth catch-ups.\par \par \uc2 Some employers feel it's important for participants to actively choose the Roth so that they are aware of how it affects their take-home pay and savings strategy, said Angela Capek, product area leader of Defined Contribution Product Platforms at \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , the nation's largest 401(k) administrator.\par \par \uc2 Capek recommends keeping an eye on your contributions to make sure your catch-ups go where they are supposed to go.\par \par \uc2 If you are in a plan that automates the switch to Roth but don't want to make \uc2 Roth\uc2  catch-up contributions, monitor your pretax contributions and change your election to zero as you approach the $24,500 regular contribution limit for 2026, Capek said.\par \par \uc2 What if my 401(k) plan doesn't offer a Roth?\par \par \uc2 High earners whose employers don't offer a Roth 401(k) won't be able to make catch-up contributions at all. Among the 401(k) plans \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  administers, 86% offered a Roth in 2024, up from 74% in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Should I stop making catch-up contributions to avoid the Roth?\par \par \uc2 At the very least, workers should understand the tax impact.\par \par \uc2 For example, a 60-year-old in the 35% tax bracket who contributes the full catch-up limit of $11,250 to a traditional 401(k) could subtract that amount from their taxable income and save nearly $4,000 in taxes. Doing the Roth instead would leave that 60-year-old with a higher adjusted gross income, which could disqualify the person from other tax breaks or push them into a higher tax bracket.\par \par \uc2 But Roths have benefits, including their tax-free withdrawals and freedom from the mandatory distributions that owners of traditional accounts are subject to.\par \par \uc2 "If you have a choice between making catch-up contributions on a Roth basis or not making them at all, it seems you are better off making the Roth contributions, especially if your goal is to get as much into your 401(k) as possible," said Ed Slott's Berger.\par \par \uc2 If Congress eventually raises tax rates, funding a Roth today could be a smart move, he said.\par \par \uc2 Why is this change happening?\par \par \uc2 The change is part of a law Congress passed in 2022, and helps the government generate upfront tax revenue to offset the cost of the law's other provisions.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260115em1f00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
