\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 AI Startup Sued by \uc2 Palantir\uc2  Denies Claims\b0\par\par\uc2 By Angel Au-Yeung \par \uc2 436 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An AI startup that \uc2 Palantir\uc2  has sued for allegedly poaching its workers and stealing company secrets has denied the allegations and asserted in a legal filing Monday that \uc2 Palantir\uc2  is trying to "scare others away from leaving."\par \par \uc2 Percepta, an artificial-intelligence company launched by two ex-\uc2 Palantir\uc2  employees and owned by venture firm \uc2 General Catalyst\uc2 , disputed \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's claims that it violated noncompetition agreements.\par \par \uc2 "In truth, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  is looking to scare others away from leaving and destroy Percepta before it can grow further," lawyers for the startup said in the filing.\par \par \uc2 In October, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  sued Percepta, alleging its two co-founders -- Hirsh Jain and Radha Jain (no relation) -- had stolen trade secrets and violated their nonsolicitation agreements. It also accused a third employee, Joanna Cohen, of stealing confidential documents from \uc2 Palantir\uc2  before she left to work for Percepta.\par \par \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's litigation joins a wave of legal battles over trade secrets and anticompetitive behavior in the AI sector. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 xAI\uc2  sued ex-employees and competitors for alleged infractions, while data-labeling startup \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  sued competitor Mercor and a former employee who left to work for Mercor for allegedly stealing trade secrets.\par \par \uc2 Palantir\uc2  is co-founded by a group that includes Chief Executive \uc2 Alex Karp\uc2  and \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 , a venture capitalist.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit against Percepta hangs on noncompetition agreements that Radha Jain and Cohen signed as part of their employment contracts with the company. Percepta said in its rebuttal that these agreements are "so broad that -- if enforced according to \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's interpretation -- it would prevent them from working for any company that uses AI to help customers or clients to solve problems anywhere in the world for 12 months."\par \par \uc2 In a previous complaint, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  accused Cohen of sending herself highly confidential documents around the time she resigned from \uc2 Palantir\uc2  in March 2025. It alleged she used her personal phone to take photographs of confidential information on her \uc2 Palantir\uc2  computer in "an obvious effort to evade \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's data system."\par \par \uc2 Percepta said in its filing that the photographs were of public-facing content drawn from \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's website. Cohen took the screenshots on her phone as a way to help herself complete tasks during her offboarding, some of which happened on two days at the start of her honeymoon, the filing said. "The photos were part of a rushed, good-faith transition after she gave notice," the Percepta attorneys said.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit asks a judge to deny \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's requests, which included enforcing Hirsh Jain, Radha Jain and Cohen's contractual obligations to \uc2 Palantir\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260114em1e0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
