\par\par \b \uc2 Target Faces Pressure Over ICE\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer and Hanna Krueger \par \uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The federal immigration crackdown in Minnesota has descended on the stores and parking lots of Target, putting the hometown retailer in a tough political spot as it faces pressure from some locals to keep border-protection agents out.\par \par \uc2 That pressure came to a head after immigration officials detained two Target employees inside the vestibule of a suburban store late last week after what appeared to be some verbal sparring in the parking lot. "I'm literally a U.S. citizen!" one shouted as they escorted him into a van.\par \par \uc2 Both are American citizens and were later released, local officials say. In a social-media post on Monday, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  said an individual in the video had been "arrested for assaulting a federal law enforcement offers [sic]."\par \par \uc2 Video of the incident quickly circulated, sparking an outcry on social media and calls from local residents and politicians for Target to protect employees and customers from agents patrolling its store lots. On Saturday, demonstrators protested outside the Richfield, Minn., store, demanding a response from Target and additional training for staff when interacting with immigration officials.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Target declined to comment, but retailers have said they can't stop law enforcement, including immigration officials from entering public areas of stores and parking lots.\par \par \uc2 Target has issued no public statement on the Richfield incident or other scenes at Target stores caught on video. In one, immigration officials leave a Target bathroom as shoppers and activists with whistles yell at them to leave the store.\par \par \uc2 As a hometown business, "it's odd for them at this moment, when Minnesota is under sort of occupation, that they are silent," said Ben Whalen, a former Richfield City Council member and part of a group that helped organize the protest.\par \par \uc2 Target is far from the only company wrestling with how to navigate the presence of immigration agents on their property. \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  parking lots have been a frequent target since the summer. Day laborers, many of them immigrants, have gathered there for years looking for temporary construction work. Some activists have asked the company to stop the raids.\par \par \uc2 In the Twin Cities area, immigration officials detained at least two people inside \uc2 Walmart\uc2  stores over the past week. Big-box store parking lots are a frequent gathering spot for immigration officials as they buy food, use the restroom and wait for commands, according to city officials and anti-\uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  activists.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 Walmart\uc2  declined to comment on the incidents in its stores or the company's law-enforcement policies.\par \par \uc2 In Richfield, a working-class suburb south of Minneapolis where around 20% of the population is Hispanic, some residents are asking how businesses can protect their customers and workers from federal immigration officials, said Michael Howard, a Minnesota state representative for the area.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has also put pressure on companies to refrain from impeding immigration officials' work. Last week, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  posted to X that Hilton had launched a "coordinated campaign" to refuse hotel rooms for \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  agents. It attached screenshots appearing to show a Hampton Inn outside Minneapolis canceling the reservations of ICE and other law-enforcement agents.\par \par \uc2 Less than 24 hours after the first posts began circulating, Hilton -- which owns the Hampton Inn brand -- removed the hotel from its system.\par \par \uc2 Target is advising store workers to stay out of the way of immigration officials or law enforcement for their own safety, one Minneapolis store worker said. The company also has reinforced that law enforcement isn't allowed in private areas, such as back offices, without a warrant, the worker said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260114em1e00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
