\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Trump Post Indirectly Revealed Hiring Stats --- Charts published Thursday evening offered early peek at some jobs figures\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Trump inadvertently posted job-market statistics online Thursday night that were supposed to remain confidential until the next day, the White House said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Presidents and their top economic advisers routinely get an advance look at big data releases, like the Labor Department's December jobs report, which was made public Friday at 8:30 a.m. in Washington, D.C. Anyone receiving the information ahead of the scheduled publication time is required by federal law to keep the figures under wraps until they are published.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday night, Trump shared charts on social media showing job totals that matched the numbers later published Friday morning, giving his online followers an early peek at some of the data.\par \par \uc2 "Following the regular procedure of presidents being pre-briefed on economic data releases, there was an inadvertent public disclosure of aggregate data that was partially derived from pre-released information," a White House official said Friday. "The White House is accordingly reviewing protocols regarding economic data releases."\par \par \uc2 "They gave me some numbers. When people give me things, I post them," Trump told reporters at the White House on Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Trump didn't post the headline numbers that economists were most eagerly awaiting Friday in the December jobs report -- net job creation of 50,000 last month and a decline in the unemployment rate to 4.4%. But his social-media posts showed job-creation totals running from early 2025 until the end of the year, indirectly revealing information from December's report.\par \par \uc2 In particular, he posted charts showing that since the end of January 2025, the economy has added 654,000 private-sector jobs but shed 181,000 government jobs, with roles in the federal government declining by 277,000. Those numbers match the totals revealed in Friday morning's jobs report, and which include job-creation tallies from December that were still confidential Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which collects the monthly employment statistics, follows strict protocols to ensure the numbers remain private until the scheduled publication. Even the agency's top official is out of the loop until a couple of days before the release. The goal is to ensure fairness across financial markets, where multitrillion-dollar markets can swing dramatically on positive or negative surprises.\par \par \uc2 One exception to the tight confidentiality is transmission of the data to the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 , whose chair typically briefs the president on the numbers the day before their release. The charts Trump posted match those typically used in such briefings.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment remains modest overall, though hiring trends have decayed steadily over the course of Trump's year back in office. The 50,000 jobs created in December undershot economists' expectations, and previously published tallies for October and November were revised lower.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260110em1a0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
