\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Private-Sector Hiring Ramps Up --- ADP says businesses in December added net 41,000 workers after November drop\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Private-sector hiring shifted back into gear in December, human-resources firm ADP estimated in its latest monthly report.\par \par \uc2 Businesses added a net 41,000 new employees last month, after shedding 29,000 on net in November, according to ADP's running estimate.\par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal had expected to see 48,000 new private-sector jobs in ADP's figures, which are derived from the anonymized payrolls of the company's corporate clients.\par \par \uc2 Big companies pulled back slightly from the job market last month, ADP chief economist \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2  said. But small businesses, which had cut roles on net in November, added to their staffs last month, putting private-sector jobs in positive territory overall, she said.\par \par \uc2 The estimate lands two days before the government's official December jobs report, hotly anticipated by analysts and investors for evidence of whether the job market continued to cool in the home stretch of last year.\par \par \uc2 Month to month, ADP's estimates sometimes vary widely from the official government data, especially before both sets of figures are later revised.\par \par \uc2 In November, the government's jobs report gave a much more upbeat view of private hiring than ADP's numbers showed. Economists are expecting the December jobs report to show that the whole economy -- not just the private sector -- added 73,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 Still, hiring has slowed markedly, leaving analysts uncertain about the economy's direction. The Labor Department's most recent figures have indicated that the 