\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Minimum-Wage Hikes Sweep the Country --- Nineteen states raised their pay floor this month, several to over $15 an hour\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jared Mitovich \par \uc2 751 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nineteen states raised their minimum wage starting this month, providing a pay bump to an estimated 8.3 million workers.\par \par \uc2 Workers in a mix of Democratic- and Republican-controlled states will see higher hourly pay this year because of automatic inflation-pegged adjustments, new laws or ballot measures. Thirty states have now set a higher minimum wage than the federal minimum of $7.25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 1, Washington state adopted the highest state-level rate at $17.13 an hour, becoming the first state to enact an hourly minimum above $17, the rate that some Democrats have pushed to be adopted nationally.\par \par \uc2 In Hawaii, baseline hourly rates rose by $2, to $16, the largest jump of any state in the country. In Nebraska and Missouri, voter-passed ballot initiatives brought those states' minimum wages up to $15 an hour, more than twice as much as some neighbors that have maintained the federal minimum of $7.25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, more workers now live in states that pay $15 or higher per hour than those that have kept the $7.25 minimum, according to an analysis by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , which estimated the number of workers affected by this year's increases.\par \par \uc2 Two states, Georgia and Wyoming, still have laws that mandate a $5.15-an-hour minimum wage, though employers in those states covered by federal labor laws have to pay the $7.25 hourly national baseline. The same is true for the five other states -- all in the South -- that don't have minimum-wage laws.\par \par \uc2 The increases come as voters grow more concerned about stubbornly high prices and the increased cost of living, and as affordability emerges as a political issue likely to play a central role in the 2026 midterm elections. More than two-thirds of people polled by Reuters/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  in October supported minimum-wage increases, including nine in 10 Democrats and nearly half of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Economists continue to debate the effects of raising the minimum wage. Some argue that setting a higher pay floor hurts businesses' bottom line, leading to increased prices or lower employment. Others say it benefits workers and reduces poverty.\par \par \uc2 David Neumark\uc2 , an economics professor at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2 , said businesses often have little maneuvering room on prices and few ways to offset the added labor costs that come with wage increases. Partly for that reason, he said, many studies show that higher minimum wages often result in fewer jobs. Other research has found no such fall in employment.\par \par \uc2 Michael Reich, an economist at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , and chair of the Center on Wage and Employment Dynamics, said price increases aren't always large, however, and don't always have a broad effect on demand. He says that was true after California enacted a $20 minimum wage for large fast-food chains in 2024, when employee turnover actually decreased.\par \par \uc2 In some places, localities have set minimum wages higher than those mandated throughout the rest of the state. Seattle's minimum wage increased to $21.30 on Jan. 1, among the highest of any city in the country. Beginning this year, workers in New York City, Long Island and New York's Westchester County will earn at least $17 an hour, higher than the $16-per-hour minimum wage recently enacted for the rest of the state. And in Los Angeles, a law passed last May set a minimum hourly pay of $30 for hotel and airport workers starting in 2028.\par \par \uc2 Smaller cities in Colorado adopted some of the largest increases in minimum worker pay this year. Edgewater, Colo., raised its minimum wage by $1.65, to $18.17. In Boulder, the minimum wage is now set at $16.82 an hour, up from $15.57.\par \par \uc2 The states that increased their minimum wage starting this month are Arizona, California, Colorado, Connecticut, Hawaii, Maine, Michigan, Minnesota, Missouri, Montana, Nebraska, New Jersey, New York, Ohio, Rhode Island, South Dakota, Vermont, Virginia and Washington.\par \par \uc2 The minimum wage in Alaska and Oregon will increase on July 1, while workers in Florida will be paid at least $15 an hour beginning Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 The federal minimum hasn't changed since 2009 -- the longest period without an adjustment since it was introduced in 1938, according to a timeline by the \uc2 U.S. Labor Department\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich451\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6765
