\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 On the Clock: Employers Use a Light Touch To Entice Workers Into Office --- Tying promotions to office attendance, increasing employee surveillance are unspoken pressures\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 999 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies' latest return-to-office push is barely noticeable. That's the point.\par \par \uc2 After waves of RTO mandates yielded mixed results, employers are betting a subtler strategy will be more effective at pulling workers back to their desks.\par \par \uc2 It's called "hybrid creep." The idea is that a combination of carrots and sticks will encourage people to gradually increase their in-office time without explicitly telling them to do so.\par \par \uc2 A pillar of this approach is tying promotions to office attendance, says human-resources consultant Jaye Johnson. Rewarding someone who shows up more than is required sends a message to the whole team.\par \par \uc2 "Let's say Sally is promoted. Now Sally is the new standard," Johnson says. "Be like Sally."\par \par \uc2 Other companies are increasing employee surveillance instead of upping their in-office requirements. They are hoping the specter of Big Brother will improve compliance with existing policies.\par \par \uc2 These unspoken pressures are all the more effective because leverage in the job market has tilted in favor of employers. Though the national unemployment rate remains low, there are roughly 700,000 more Americans out of work than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 And with layoffs on the rise, it doesn't necessarily take a decree for workers to understand that spending more time in the office could be a key to staying employed.\par \par \uc2 "We're in a climate where people are afraid of losing their jobs," Johnson says. "So, if there's an office nearby, I'm going to be there whether I want to or not."\par \par \uc2 She advises managers to be open about their expectations. That could mean telling ambitious employees that exceeding the in-office minimum is part of the promotion criteria.\par \par \uc2 It's only fair for people to know how the game is played, she figures, but some bosses prefer unwritten rules.\par \par \uc2 Workers seem to be taking the hint. Office attendance rates continue to tick up even as we reach a mandate plateau. This suggests people are responding to other factors.\par \par \uc2 Badge-swipe company \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2  has a back-to-work barometer, an estimate of weekly average attendance. It has posted year-over-year gains in each of the past six months.\par \par \uc2 Notably, more than 50% attendance is now the norm, which wasn't the case last year.\par \par \uc2 Weekly averages tell only part of the story. In recent years, attendance has varied widely from day to day, with employees congregating midweek and often working remotely on either side of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Those work-from-home Mondays and Fridays are eroding.\par \par \uc2 Commercial real-estate firm \uc2 JLL\uc2  analyzed five years of office attendance in major U.S. cities and found its buildings have been 56% full on Fridays and 67% full on Mondays this year.\par \par \uc2 The attendance rate on Mondays is now as high as it was on Wednesdays in 2023. Wednesday has long been the most popular in-office day. \uc2 JLL\uc2  offices are now almost as full on Fridays as they were on Wednesdays in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Slowly but surely, workers are surrendering some of the WFH freedom that once eased them into and out of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 JLL\uc2  uses mobile-phone location data to measure office attendance. Tracking has gotten more precise, which partly explains why workers are showing up more often, says Stanford economist \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 , who studies hybrid work.\par \par \uc2 Many companies initially relied solely on badge swipes to track attendance, and it was relatively easy to game the system. "Coffee badging" -- going to the office just long enough to show your face and grab a cup o' joe -- was a popular workaround.\par \par \uc2 And because it is common for a few employees to walk in together, with only one swiping a badge to open the door, HR departments were reluctant to accuse anyone of playing hooky.\par \par \uc2 Businesses now know with greater confidence who is present, and employees are aware of this.\par \par \uc2 "It's like speed cameras on freeways," Bloom says. "The better enforcement technology makes folks obey speed limits, rather than drivers suddenly wanting to drive slowly. Same thing with office attendance."\par \par \uc2 To be fair, some people are creeping back to the office because they missed it.\par \par \uc2 Tom Enright works in person at least once a week, even though he is required to show up only once a month to the Charlotte, N.C., office of the staffing firm where he is a manager. He says he thrives on face-to-face energy and doesn't feel pressured. If anything, his example influences others.\par \par \uc2 "I'd been saying for a while I was going to go in more often but I wasn't, so I realized I've got to put a day of the week on the calendar and let everyone know," says Enright, 60. "Once I started doing that, I realized there were other people doing the same thing."\par \par \uc2 The software company \uc2 Cloudera\uc2  doesn't require employees to work a certain number of days in the office. Nevertheless, attendance has climbed 22% since 2022, says Chief People Officer Amy Nelson. Many engineers show up two to four days a week and stay five or six hours. In the recent past, it was common to come in for a meeting and then leave.\par \par \uc2 "Advancement is based on results, leadership behaviors and contributions to the business," Nelson tells me. "With that said, visibility and collaboration does matter in any organization."\par \par \uc2 That's a good reminder for anyone thinking about upping his or her in-office game. If the bare minimum is enough for you to be seen and recognized for solid work, there might be no need to become an office overachiever.\par \par \uc2 But if you observe that the co-workers who stand out are the ones with the most face time, it could be worth pulling on real pants and pulling into the office parking lot. Even if it's Friday.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260105em150000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
