\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Behind the Making of a Larger-Than-Life Game Show --- The man known as MrBeast figured out how to go viral. Now he's aiming bigger.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ashley Wong \par \uc2 1,775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 January 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Greenville, N.C. -- Nine people are being placed in coffins with provisions including pillows, carbon-dioxide monitors and snacks, then lowered into the earth and covered with packed dirt.\par \par \uc2 This isn't a torture scene but a challenge being filmed for the second season of the Prime Video series "Beast Games," a cornerstone of the massive entertainment empire commanded by Jimmy Donaldson -- the YouTuber better known as MrBeast. At stake is a $5 million cash prize.\par \par \uc2 "I've always wanted to do the biggest game show in history," Donaldson says when we meet in August, stretched out on a couch at his Greenville production studio.\par \par \uc2 The 27-year-old YouTuber has been buried alive multiple times, so for him this is business as usual. Since launching his \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel in 2012, Donaldson has recreated "Squid Game" in real life, spent seven days in solitary confinement, chained two exes together and challenged them to live in the same house for 30 days and raced an actual cheetah against a Formula E race car. His videos feature giant cash giveaways, dramatic explosions and famous faces such as Cristiano Ronaldo and Justin Timberlake.\par \par \uc2 He's often referred to as the man who cracked the secret to going viral: With over 450 million subscribers, he now runs the most popular \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel in the world.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, Donaldson made his first foray into traditional entertainment with "Beast Games." The reality show featured 1,000 people competing over the course of 10 episodes for a $10 million prize. Contestants were hunted by Navy SEALs on a private island and subjected to a confinement experiment, among other challenges.\par \par \uc2 As Gen Z and Gen Alpha increasingly define consumer tastes, these kinds of high-stakes antics are charting a new course for entertainment.\par \par \uc2 "His commitment to his craft is no different than an Olympic athlete getting up at 4 in the morning to be on the river to row," says Jeff Housenbold, the chief executive of Beast Industries, who joined the company in 2024. "It's that discipline mentally, physically, that maniacal focus that makes you the best in the world."\par \par \uc2 Season 1 of "Beast Games" was Prime Video's most-watched unscripted show of all time, according to \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , hitting 50 million viewers within the first 25 days after it aired. It was such a hit that North Carolina Gov. Josh Stein announced last year that the state was giving the show a $15 million grant to film the second season there. (The first season was filmed mostly in Toronto.) Season 3 is supposed to begin production in late spring or early summer, a person familiar with the show said.\par \par \uc2 The show is still battling a lawsuit filed by a handful of season 1 contestants who alleged unpleasant conditions during a portion filmed in Las Vegas that only aired on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . The plaintiffs allege that the production violated labor laws and provided inadequate food and drink, among other claims, and are seeking financial compensation.\par \par \uc2 Some of the claims hinge on the idea that the plaintiffs should be classified as employees of the show, rather than independent contractors, who have different legal protections. A legal representative for MrBeast said in a statement: "Beast Industries does not comment on active litigation, but we can confidently confirm the contestants in Beast Games are indeed contestants, and recognized as such under States' contests law and federal law, so we will vigorously defend to preserve the Beast Games contests."\par \par \uc2 Robert Pafundi, who represents the contestants, emphasized "this litigation is very much ongoing."\par \par \uc2 The first season was a learning curve in other ways for the MrBeast team. Some viewers said they had a hard time connecting with individual players and their story lines, the result of having 1,000 contestants. Viewers also criticized the number of games that were left up to chance.\par \par \uc2 In season 2, Donaldson said, the team took that feedback to heart and cut back on some of the chance-based games, like vote-based eliminations, and emphasized skill-based challenges like memorization and rope climbing where the wins could feel more earned.\par \par \uc2 They also cut down the number of players, starting the second season with 200 contestants before quickly whittling them down to 100, then 25.\par \par \uc2 "That took everything in my soul to do, because my natural intuition is to go bigger. Like, '10,000 people in season 2!'" Donaldson says.\par \par \uc2 The theme of season 2 is "Strong vs. Smart," pitting 100 athletically inclined contestants against 100 contestants who run on brainpower.\par \par \uc2 Season 2 also features a crossover episode with "Survivor," the long-running reality show that helped inspire "Beast Games." The crew took players to one of the Fijian islands where "Survivor" is filmed, and legendary "Survivor" host Jeff Probst himself leads the episode alongside Donaldson.\par \par \uc2 Watching Probst and Donaldson side-by-side is like watching a master teach his pupil in real time. In the crossover episode, Probst gently reminds Donaldson, who is known for yelling until his voice cracks during videos, that people in charge don't always need to scream. Donaldson took notes on Probst's techniques for getting deep answers out of contestants and the way he allowed discomfort or tension between contestants to naturally develop into drama on camera.\par \par \uc2 "It's not like I got acting lessons or had a coach," Donaldson says. "I've been making \uc2 YouTube\uc2  videos since I was 11, and I'm not really around other reality-show hosts. So it was a really good experience for me."\par \par \uc2 Probst filmed his "Beast Games" appearance over the course of one day, which he squeezed in while also working on "Survivor" season 50. Both shows, he said, reveal how people will act under unusual forms of pressure.\par \par \uc2 "What's really sophisticated is that Jimmy uses that spectacle as a kind of disguise," Probst said. "It's big, loud, colorful and incredibly entertaining, but underneath that is something much more personal."\par \par \uc2 To house the contestants, Donaldson and his team built "Beast City." It was a fully functioning ecosystem where contestants slept, ate, played, read and whiled away the hours, built in a stretch of empty field off a Greenville highway. There was a basketball court, wading pools, a library, a terrarium, chess tables, a gym, bicycles and a snack room filled entirely with Feastables products.\par \par \uc2 There was even a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stand manned by actual \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas. They worked the same night hours as the contestants, who could order as many iced coffees or sandwich wraps as they wanted.\par \par \uc2 In the center of it all, under a Plexiglas box atop a glowing tower, sat the $5 million cash prize -- stacks and stacks of real dollar bills.\par \par \uc2 Donaldson and Housenbold have continued to build the more traditional entertainment arm of Beast Industries.\par \par \uc2 The company announced in September that Corie Henson would become the first president of Beast Industry Studios, overseeing long-form and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  content. Henson has spent more than a decade working in unscripted television at multiple networks, most recently as head of unscripted content at NBC. Before she was approached for the job, she says, she had watched a few MrBeast videos and even received some pitches from his studio while at NBC.\par \par \uc2 "I would love to help him become more four-quadrant," Henson said, the industry's term for content that appeals to the widest variety of audiences possible. "I would love for not only my kids and my friends who have kids to be watching the videos. I want their moms to be sitting down with them going, 'Oh, what did MrBeast do this week?'"\par \par \uc2 Other Beast ventures in the works include a mobile-phone plan called Beast Mobile and Beast Financial, a financial-planning app that sounds a bit like \uc2 Robinhood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donaldson also juggles a loose grab bag of philanthropic efforts. Some of these efforts have drawn scrutiny, as some have become fodder for his \uc2 YouTube\uc2  videos; critics say he exploits tragedy for views and profit. But Donaldson sees these videos as pragmatic; making money and promoting charitable causes, he and his team would argue, are not diametrically opposed. He has spearheaded efforts to plant trees, clean up oceans, rivers and beaches, pay for strangers' cataract surgeries and prosthetic limbs and has donated millions to charities such as Make-A-Wish and food banks. In November, he announced a partnership with \uc2 the Rockefeller Foundation\uc2 , joining forces to encourage young people to engage in and learn more about philanthropy.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Donaldson launched Feastables, a snack line mostly known for its chocolate bars. Donaldson gets most of his cocoa from Tony's Open Chain, the supply chain behind chocolate brand Tony's Chocolonely. The chain works to pay farmers a living wage and establish fair labor conditions in the West African countries most cocoa comes from. His goal, Donaldson has said, is to eventually get 100% of his cocoa from fair-labor cocoa farms and eliminate child labor in the cocoa industry.\par \par \uc2 There was also a 45-day pop-up MrBeast-themed amusement park in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, called Beast Land. The park, which closed in late December, contains games and rides that reference past MrBeast videos, such as a trapdoor tower and catapults.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia has worked to expand its cultural influence in recent years, launching film festivals, comedy festivals, the Esports World Cup and securing the host nation spot for the 2034 FIFA World Cup. The Kingdom has drawn criticism for its human-rights record under the crown prince. Some critics have accused the country of using major cultural events to try to polish its image.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the criticism, Donaldson declines to comment. "This was a unique opportunity to join a significant movement of leading brands and talent who share the belief that an exchange of ideas and cultures is better for the world," a spokesperson for Beast Industries said.\par \par \uc2 Last November, Donaldson wrote on X that his latest \uc2 YouTube\uc2  videos weren't meeting his bar for entertainment. "Ya boy is going to go into ultra grind mode and make the greatest content of my life in 2026," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 A few of the videos he had put out around that time just didn't feel groundbreaking to him, he said. As his empire continues to expand, he said he wanted to publicly announce his commitment to pushing the envelope on the platform where everything began.\par \par \uc2 "I just want to make sure me and the team aren't getting lazy," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260103em1300017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
